const examData = [
    {
        id: 1,
        topic: "Third Parties / Delegation of Duties",
        fp: "In exchange for use of his neighbor's ski house, the homeowner hired a lawn service to maintain his lawn and his neighbor's lawn for the next two years, agreeing to pay the lawn service $50 per month for certain specified services. The homeowner and the lawn service signed a memorandum of agreement, and the homeowner gave a photocopy of it to his neighbor. Two months later, the homeowner sold his house to a doctor. At the closing of title, the homeowner handed the doctor the original memorandum of his agreement with the lawn service and executed an assignment to the doctor of his rights thereunder. The lawn service continued rendering services as agreed for an additional six months, receiving a check for $50 from the doctor each month. Then, the lawn service notified the neighbor and the doctor that it would no longer be able to work on their lawns.\n\nThe neighbor hired a gardener to continue performing the same services on the neighbor's lawn for a fee of $25 per month. If the neighbor sues the doctor for $400 ($25 per month for the 16 months remaining in the term), should the court rule in the neighbor's favor?",
        q: "If the neighbor sues the doctor for $400 ($25 per month for the 16 months remaining in the term), should the court rule in the neighbor's favor?",
        opts: [
            "Yes, because the doctor's monthly payments to the lawn service resulted in an estoppel-type waiver of his rights to deny liability.",
            "Yes, because the homeowner's promise to provide lawn maintenance services touches and concerns the land.",
            "No, because the doctor did not agree to pay for the maintenance of the neighbor's lawn.",
            "No, because the price of $25 per month to maintain the neighbor's lawn was equal to one-half the price that the lawn service charged for maintaining both lawns."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "An assignment transfers rights under a contract, but it does not transfer duties. A transfer of duties is a delegation. Although an assignment of a contract that is still executory on both sides will usually be construed as implying a delegation of duties as well as an assignment of rights, a delegation does not make the delegatee liable for performance unless he or she agreed to assume those duties. The doctor's acceptance of the assignment did not obligate him to pay for the maintenance of the neighbor's lawn because he never agreed to assume that duty.\n\nA is incorrect because the doctor's monthly payments did not waive his rights to deny liability. B is incorrect because the homeowner's promise to provide lawn maintenance was personal and did not run with the land. D is incorrect because the cost of the neighbor's replacement services is irrelevant to the question of the doctor's liability."
    },
    {
        id: 2,
        topic: "Performance / Anticipatory Repudiation (Non-Delegable Duties)",
        fp: "On April 1, a law firm hired a famous and uniquely skilled software specialist to design and install new computer software specifically for the firm. The new software was to be installed by October 1. On August 15, after winning the state lottery, the specialist sold her business to a computer dealer. The computer dealer agreed to fulfill any existing contracts entered into by the specialist.\n\nOn August 16, the specialist informed the firm of her sale to the dealer, stating that the software called for by the contract would be designed and installed by the dealer and not the specialist. If the firm institutes a claim against the specialist on August 18, should the court rule in its favor?",
        q: "If the firm institutes a claim against the specialist on August 18, should the court rule in its favor?",
        opts: [
            "Yes, because the specialist's statement on August 16 was an anticipatory repudiation.",
            "Yes, because the specialist's sale to the dealer did not impose on the dealer an obligation to design software.",
            "No, because design and installation of the software was not required until October 1.",
            "No, because her sale to the dealer implied a delegation of all her obligations under her contract with the firm."
        ],
        ans: 0,
        exp: "An anticipatory repudiation occurs when a party to a contract clearly indicates to the other party that he or she will not perform as agreed. Contract duties that involve personal services or unique skills are not delegable without the consent of the obligee. Because the software specialist was hired for her unique and famous skills, her duties were non-delegable. Her statement that the dealer, and not she, would design and install the software was an unlawful delegation and a clear manifestation that she would not perform her personal service obligation, constituting an immediate anticipatory repudiation. The firm was entitled to treat this as an immediate total breach and sue on August 18.\n\nB is incorrect because the dealer did agree to fulfill existing contracts. C is incorrect because an anticipatory repudiation allows the non-breaching party to bring an immediate action for damages before the performance date. D is incorrect because an obligor cannot delegate non-delegable personal service duties without consent."
    },
    {
        id: 3,
        topic: "Defenses / Statute of Frauds (Merchant's Confirmatory Memo)",
        fp: "On May 15, a music store telephoned a guitar factory and ordered 50 guitars at a price of $200 per guitar. On May 16, the music store sent a signed letter marked 'Confirmation' that read: 'This confirms our order of 5 guitars.' On May 17, the factory shipped 50 guitars to the store. On May 18, the factory received the letter from the music store. On May 19, the factory sent a bill to the store for $10,000. On May 20, the music store received the bill. On May 21, the music store sent back the 50 guitars with a letter marked 'Failure to Order' that read: 'We never ordered these.' On May 22, the factory received the letter. The factory can make all of the guitars it has customer orders for, and it makes a profit of $50 per guitar.\n\nMay the factory recover on the contract against the music store?",
        q: "May the factory recover on the contract against the music store?",
        opts: [
            "Yes, for $10,000.",
            "Yes, for $2,500.",
            "Yes, for $250.",
            "No."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "Under UCC § 2-201(2), between merchants, if within a reasonable time a writing in confirmation of the contract and sufficient against the sender is received and the party receiving it has reason to know its contents, it satisfies the requirements of the Statute of Frauds against the recipient unless written notice of objection is given within 10 days. However, under UCC § 2-201(1), the contract is not enforceable under this exception beyond the quantity of goods shown in such writing. Because the confirmation letter signed by the music store stated a quantity of only 5 guitars, the contract is enforceable only to the extent of 5 guitars. Because the factory is a lost volume seller, its damages are measured by its lost profits ($50 per guitar). For 5 guitars, the factory is entitled to recover $250.\n\nA is incorrect because the contract cannot be enforced for the full $10,000 price since the goods were rightfully rejected and the quantity term in the confirmation was only 5. B is incorrect because enforcing a contract for 50 guitars would violate the Statute of Frauds quantity limitation in § 2-201(1). D is incorrect because the confirmation satisfied the Statute of Frauds up to 5 guitars."
    },
    {
        id: 4,
        topic: "Formation / Privity of Contract",
        fp: "A local school held a charity auction where companies and other members of the community donated items for the school to auction off. A local guitar maker agreed to donate a new electric guitar valued at over $1,500. During the auction, the plaintiff bid $50 and was declared the highest bidder. The plaintiff gave the school a check for $50. Because the guitar company was so embarrassed at the low price paid for the guitar, it refused to deliver it to the plaintiff. If the plaintiff asserts a claim against the guitar company for failing to deliver the guitar, should the court rule in the plaintiff's favor?",
        q: "If the plaintiff asserts a claim against the guitar company for failing to deliver the guitar, should the court rule in the plaintiff's favor?",
        opts: [
            "Yes, as an assignee.",
            "Yes, as an intended third-party creditor beneficiary.",
            "No, because there was no agreement between it and the plaintiff.",
            "No, because the agreement was not supported by consideration."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "A promise to make a gift to a charity is generally unenforceable for lack of consideration unless the charity detrimentally relies on the promise under promissory estoppel or an applicable statute renders charitable subscriptions binding. Here, the action is by the bidder against the donor guitar company. The guitar company's agreement with the school was an unexecuted, gratuitous promise to make a gift. Because the company received no consideration from the school or the plaintiff for its promise to donate the guitar, the promise was unenforceable. Furthermore, the plaintiff contracted only with the school at the auction, not with the donor.\n\nA is incorrect because the school did not assign its rights to the plaintiff. B is incorrect because the school owed no preexisting obligation to the plaintiff that would make the plaintiff a creditor beneficiary of the donation agreement. C is incorrect because lack of privity alone would not bar an intended third-party beneficiary if the underlying contract were valid and supported by consideration."
    },
    {
        id: 5,
        topic: "Excuses / Frustration of Purpose",
        fp: "A famous musician was scheduled to play a concert that would be broadcast on national television. A music-streaming service that had an exclusive agreement with the musician contracted with the television network to run a 30-second advertisement on Saturday, October 1, at 8:15 P.M. Although the contract did not state it specifically, the understanding was that the advertisement would run during the last 15 minutes of the concert. The advertisement was designed to promote the music-streaming service by advertising the musician's 'surprise' new album that was exclusive to the site.\n\nThe day before the concert, the musician was killed in a car accident. The television station offered to run the advertisement at the agreed time for a 20 percent discount, but the music service declined, stating that the entire advertisement and advertising campaign was built around the concert occurring. If the television station sues the music-streaming service for breach of contract, should the court rule in the television station's favor?",
        q: "If the television station sues the music-streaming service for breach of contract, should the court rule in the television station's favor?",
        opts: [
            "Yes, because the contract did not specifically state the advertisement would run during the concert.",
            "Yes, since there was nothing stopping it from running the advertisement at the agreed time.",
            "No, under the doctrine of impossibility.",
            "No, under the doctrine of frustration of purpose."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "Under the doctrine of frustration of purpose, where, after a contract is made, a party's principal purpose is substantially frustrated without his fault by the occurrence of an event the non-occurrence of which was a basic assumption on which the contract was made, his remaining duties to render performance are discharged, unless the language or circumstances indicate the contrary. Here, both parties mutually understood that the primary purpose of the commercial was to promote the artist's surprise album during the live concert broadcast. The untimely death of the musician and cancellation of the concert completely destroyed the value and principal purpose of the advertisement, discharging the music-streaming service under frustration of purpose.\n\nA and B are incorrect because frustration of purpose applies even if literal performance (airing the commercial) remains physically possible. C is incorrect because performance was not objectively impossible, but rather the underlying purpose of the contract was frustrated."
    },
    {
        id: 6,
        topic: "Defenses / Mutual Mistake",
        fp: "During an ice storm, a man's car slipped down an embankment and became lodged against a large tree. The man called a towing company and told the company's manager that the car was 100 feet down the embankment. 'That's lucky,' said the manager, 'because our winch only goes 100 feet.' After the manager and the man agreed on a price, an employee of the company attempted to reach the car but could not because the car turned out to be 120 feet down the embankment.",
        q: "Is the towing company's performance excused on the grounds of mistake?",
        opts: [
            "No, because both parties were uncertain about the distance.",
            "No, because the towing company assumed the risk by the manager's failure to examine the distance himself.",
            "Yes, because at the time of contracting, both parties were mistaken about a basic assumption on which the contract was based.",
            "Yes, because the agreement did not allocate the risk of mistake to either party."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because either party may have its performance excused under the doctrine of mutual mistake if both parties were mistaken about a basic assumption on which the contract was based.\n\nWhenever you have a fact pattern where at least one party seems to be mistaken about some fact concerning the deal, the first thing you need to do is to check whether it's only one party, or rather both parties, that have made the mistake (because the rules differ considerably between the two situations). Here, both parties had the same mistake of fact about how many feet down the embankment the car was. In this 'mutual mistake' situation, you can and should use the following two rules from the Restatement (2d) of Contracts for determining whether the contract is voidable:\n\n§ 152 When Mistake of Both Parties Makes a Contract Voidable\n(1) Where a mistake of both parties at the time a contract was made as to a basic assumption on which the contract was made has a material effect on the agreed exchange of performances, the contract is voidable by the adversely affected party unless he bears the risk of the mistake under the rule stated in § 154.\n\n§ 154 When a Party Bears the Risk of a Mistake\nA party bears the risk of a mistake when\n(a) the risk is allocated to him by agreement of the parties, or\n(b) he is aware, at the time the contract is made, that he has only limited knowledge with respect to the facts to which the mistake relates but treats his limited knowledge as sufficient, or\n(c) the risk is allocated to him by the court on the ground that it is reasonable in the circumstances to do so.\n\nAs you can see, the underlined words from Rest. (2d) § 152(1) ('basic assumption on which the contract was made') correspond precisely to the words in Choice (C). And the parties' initial discussion of the fact that 'our winch only goes 100 feet' certainly makes the depth of the embankment a 'basic assumption on which the contract was made.' Therefore, § 152 tells us that (C) will be correct 'unless [the adversely affected party] bears the risk of the mistake under the rule stated in § 154.'\n\nSo let's look at the Rest. (2d) § 154 'bears the risk' rule. Sub-part (a) clearly doesn't apply, since the parties have not agreed about which of them, if either, bear the risk of such a mistake. (Indeed, since the key information about depth of embankment came from the man, a court is more likely to find that the parties implicitly agreed to allocate the risk to the man than that they agreed to allocated to the towing company.) Sub-part (b) also doesn't seem to apply, because even if the towing company was aware that it had 'only limited knowledge with respect to the facts to which the mistake relates,' nothing the company said suggests that it 'treat[ed its] limited knowledge as sufficient.' And as to sub-part (c), it's very unlikely that a court would conclude that allocating the risk to the towing company is 'reasonable in the circumstances,' given that the information came from the man, and the towing company had no ability to verify the correctness of the information at the time it entered into the contract (during the phone call). Therefore, the 'unless he bears the risk' portion of Restatement §§ 152 and 154 does not come into play, so the towing company is excused because of this mutual mistake about a basic assumption.\n\n(A) is not the best response, because the fact that both parties were uncertain about the distance is not by itself sufficient to allow the company to be excused on the grounds of mistake. As we discuss in Choice (C), the doctrine of mutual mistake can allow either party to be excused from performance. But for that doctrine to apply, the situation must meet several requirements. One is that the parties share a 'mistake'; the fact that the parties share an 'uncertainty' about a factual matter does not mean that they both made a 'mistake' about that matter. For instance, if the man had told the company, 'I'm not sure how far down the embankment my car is,' and the company agreed to perform notwithstanding this uncertainty, the doctrine of mutual mistake would not apply. It's only because the two parties shared the same 'mistake'—i.e., the same actual belief that was not in accordance with the facts—that the doctrine of mutual mistake became available. (And even then, the doctrine would have required not just that both parties shared a particular mistake, but that (a) the mistake was one as to 'a basic assumption on which the contract was made'; and (b) the mistake was not one as to which that party bore the risk. Choice (A) doesn't mention either of these requirements, still another reason making this choice not the best response.)\n\n(B) is not the best response, because the towing company justifiably relied on the man's estimate of the distance, and therefore did not assume the risk of a mistake about that fact. As we discuss in connection with Choice (C), under the rules for deciding when a mutual mistake entitles a party to avoid the contract, there are indeed circumstances in which a party will be denied the right to avoid the contract because that party is deemed to have assumed the risk of the type of error in question. The circumstances are listed in Restatement (2d) § 154, quoted in the discussion of Choice (C). But none of the three circumstances that would cause the towing company to be found to have assumed the risk of this mistake ((a) the risk was allocated to it by agreement between the parties; (b) the company was aware that it had only limited knowledge but treated its limited knowledge as sufficient; or (c) the risk was allocated to it by the court because this was reasonable in the circumstances)) applies here. The mere fact that the manager could in theory have 'examined the distance himself' before committing to the contract is not by itself enough to trigger any of the three reasons for making the company bear this risk.\n\n(D) is not the best response, because even if the agreement did not allocate the risk of mistake to either party (and it probably did not make such an allocation), that would by itself not be enough to entitle the company to have the contract excused on the grounds of mistake. As we discuss in connection with Choice (C), the doctrine of mutual mistake allows one party to void the contract if both parties are mistaken about a 'basic assumption on which the contract was made' (assuming that the agreement did not allocate the risk of mistake to that party). It's not enough that the parties made a mutual mistake the risk of which was not allocated to either party; the mistake must be one about a 'basic assumption on which the contract was made.' Since this choice implicitly asserts that any mutual mistake - even if it's about a minor assumption—will allow avoidance if the agreement did not allocate the risk of mistake to one party, the choice does not correctly reflect the requirements for avoidance. This choice thus gives a legally incorrect reason for the correct outcome."
    },
    {
        id: 7,
        topic: "Defenses / Fraudulent Misrepresentation and Reformation",
        fp: "An employer offered to pay a terminated employee $50,000 to release all claims the employee might have against the employer. The employee orally accepted the offer. The employer then prepared an unsigned release agreement and sent it to the employee for him to sign. The employee carefully prepared, signed, and sent to the employer a substitute release agreement that was identical to the original except that it excluded from the release any age discrimination claims. The employer signed the substitute release without reading it. Shortly thereafter, the employee notified the employer that he intended to sue the employer for age discrimination.",
        q: "Is the employer likely to prevail in an action seeking reformation of the release to conform to the parties' oral agreement?",
        opts: [
            "No, because the employer acted unreasonably by failing to read the substitute release prior to signing it.",
            "No, because the parol evidence rule will preclude evidence of the oral agreement.",
            "Yes, because the employee's fraudulent behavior induced the employer's unilateral mistake.",
            "Yes, because the parties were mutually mistaken regarding the contents of the signed release."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because the employee's failure to disclose the change he made to the release constituted misrepresentation, making reformation appropriate even though the employer did not read the release before signing it.\n\nFor the employer to prevail, it will have to overcome three major obstacles: (1) the employer will have to show why the parol evidence rule does not exclude evidence of the oral agreement (which included a complete release of all claims); (2) even after the employer satisfies (1), it will have to show why it is entitled to reformation (which in this case will require it to show fraud / intentional misrepresentation) by the employee); and (3) the employer will have to show why its own failure to read the substitute release before signing it shouldn't deprive it of a reformation that it would otherwise be entitled to. But the employer will, in fact, be able to overcome all three obstacles, and will thus be entitled to reformation.\n\nAs to obstacle (1) (the parol evidence rule), an important exception to the rule is that does not bar proof that the writing was procured by fraud. See Farnsworth Treatise (4th Ed.), § 7.5, p. 434: 'The parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of prior negotiations to show either mistake or fraud as a basis for reformation. Such evidence is admissible even though its purpose is to show that the writing does not properly express the agreement that was reached[.]'\n\nAs to obstacle (2) (right to reformation), the employer's best—indeed only—chance to show why it's entitled to reformation is to show that the employee's substitution of the partial release for the full one constituted fraud. To do this, the employer will have to accomplish two sub-steps: (a) the employer will have to show that if the employee's substitution constituted fraud or misrepresentation, this conduct would entitle the employer to reformation; and (b) the employer will have to show that the employee's conduct—which did not include any express misrepresentation—nonetheless constituted fraudulent misrepresentation. Let's take each sub-step in turn.\n\nAs to sub-step (a): Where one party's intentional misrepresentation (i.e., fraud) about the contents or effects of a writing causes the other party to agree to the writing, the latter is entitled to reformation. Thus Restatement (2d) of Contracts § 166 (entitled 'When a Misrepresentation as to a Writing Justifies Reformation') says that: 'If a party's manifestation of assent is induced by the other party's fraudulent misrepresentation as to the contents or effect of a writing evidencing or embodying in whole or in part an agreement, the court at the request of the recipient may reform the writing to express the terms of the agreement as asserted, (a) if the recipient was justified in relying on the misrepresentation [.]' Applying this Restatement principle, if the employer can show (1) that the employee made a fraudulent misrepresentation as to the 'contents of a writing... embodying [their] agreement,' and (2) that it (the employer) was 'justified in relying on the misrepresentation,' then the court may (indeed will) 'reform the writing to express the terms of the agreement' to match the terms that the employee fraudulently misrepresented the contents of the writing to be. Here, there's no question that (1) the employee, by substituting the release and sending the signed substitute back to the employer, was implicitly representing something about the 'contents or effect' of the signed release he sent (that it was the same one as the unsigned draft that the employer had sent the employee to sign); and (2) the employer was 'justified in relying' on this implicit representation.\n\nAs to sub-step (b) (that the employee's conduct constituted fraudulent misrepresentation): the obvious challenge that the employer must meet is that the employee did not make any explicit misrepresentation (or, indeed, any explicit representation, 'mis' or otherwise)—all the employee did was to send back, without comment, the substitute release with his signature on it. Normally, a 'fraudulent misrepresentation' will be an explicit (and intentional) misrepresentation of something. But in a few special scenarios, a person's mere failure to disclose a fact will be treated as being equivalent to an assertion that the fact does not exist. Here's how this principle is articulated (as edited to show only the part that's relevant here) in the Second Restatement: § 161 When Non-Disclosure Is Equivalent to an Assertion. A person's non-disclosure of a fact known to him is equivalent to an assertion that the fact does not exist in the following cases only..... (c) where he knows that disclosure of the fact would correct a mistake of the other party as to the contents or effect of a writing, evidencing or embodying an agreement in whole or in part. You can see how this principle applies here: (1) there is a fact (that the employee has prepared and is enclosing a substitute release that excludes age-discrimination), which fact is 'known to [the employee]'; (2) the employee knows that the employer, having prepared the original release, will almost certainly labor under a mistaken belief that the 'contents... of [the substitute] writing [signed by the employee] evidencing or embodying an agreement' are the same as the contents of the original writing sent by the employer to the employee; (3) the employee knows that if he were to disclose this fact (that what he is enclosing is a substitute release without the age-discrimination clause), that disclosure would 'correct [the] mistake' as to the 'content or effect of [the] writing'; and therefore (4) the employee's 'non-disclosure of [this] fact known to him' (that he had changed the contents of the release) 'is equivalent to an assertion that the fact does not exist' (i.e., equivalent to an assertion that the document he has signed and returned is the same one that the employer originally sent him). Choice (C)—by saying that the employee's 'fraudulent behavior induced the employer's unilateral mistake'—effectively captures the long chain of reasoning above.\n\n(A) is not the best response, because the employer's failure to read the substitute release prior to signing it was not unreasonable, given that the employee fraudulently induced the employer to sign without reading. As we saw in the discussion of Choice (C), where a party's assent to a written agreement is 'induced by the other party's fraudulent misrepresentation as to the contents... of [the] writing,' the recipient of the misrepresentation is entitled to reformation 'if the recipient was justified in relying on the misrepresentation.' Choice (A) is therefore based on a correct legal principle: if the employer (the recipient of the substitute release) indeed 'acted unreasonably by failing to read the substitute release prior to signing it' then, even given that the employee's silence amounted to misrepresentation (see the discussion of Choice (C)), the employer would not have been 'justified in relying' on the misrepresentation when it could have learned of the release's true contents by reading it. However, in this case the employer did not 'act unreasonably' by failing to read the substitute release. Restatement (2d) of Contracts § 172 (entitled 'When Fault Makes Reliance Unjustified') says that in cases where a party seeks reformation on account of the other party's misrepresentation as to a writing (the basic Rest. 2d § 166 situation that governs this problem): 'A recipient's fault in not knowing or discovering the facts before making the contract does not make his reliance unjustified unless it amounts to a failure to act in good faith and in accordance with reasonable standards of fair dealing.' This is a very tough standard for the party resisting reformation to meet; Comment a to Rest. § 172 says that 'The recipient's fault makes his reliance unjustified only in extreme cases where he has failed to act in good faith and in accordance with reasonable standards of fair dealing.' Illustration 1 to § 172 involves a fact pattern much like this one: after A and B make an oral agreement contemplating a letter writing, 'A prepares a writing containing essential terms different from those agreed upon and induces B to sign it by telling him that it contains the agreed terms and that it is not necessary for him to read it.' The illustration concludes that B's reliance on A's statement 'is justified since his fault does not amount to a failure to act in good faith and in accordance with reasonable standards of fair dealing.... [B] may have the writing reformed.' Here, similarly, the employer's fault in not reading the substitute release 'does not amount to a failure to act in good faith and in accordance with reasonable standards of fair dealing,' so Choice (A)'s factual assertion that 'the employer acted unreasonably' is not accurate.\n\n(B) is not the best response, because the employee's fraud prevents the parol evidence rule from applying. This choice hints at a correct rule of law: the parol evidence rule will indeed often preclude evidence of a prior oral agreement when the parties have later signed a final writing that is inconsistent with the alleged oral agreement. However, as we note in the discussion of Choice (C), the parol evidence rule does not bar evidence of prior negotiations to show that the written agreement was procured by fraud. This choice, by asserting that the rule 'will preclude evidence of the oral agreement,' ignores the procured-by-fraud exception, which is applicable on these facts as we describe in the treatment of Choice (C).\n\n(D) is not the best response, because only one party (the employer), not both, was mutually mistaken concerning the contents of the signed release. It's clear from the statement of facts that the employee knew that the contents of the substitute (and signed) release were different than those of the release tendered by the employer; we're told that the employee 'carefully prepared' the substitute release, so he must have known that he was excluding age discrimination from the coverage of the release. This choice, by asserting that 'the parties were mutually mistaken,' is therefore factually incorrect. Consequently, the choice gives an incorrect explanation for the correct outcome (that reformation is available)."
    },
    {
        id: 8,
        topic: "Formation / Implied-in-Fact Contract",
        fp: "An attorney received a document at his office with an attached note from a client for whom he had just finished drafting a will. The note read as follows: 'Do you think this contract of sale for my boat complies with state law? I would have talked to you in person about this, but I'm on my way out of town. I will be back next week.' The attorney reviewed the document and wrote a one-page letter to the client stating that the document complied with state law. The lawyer included a bill for $500, which was a reasonable fee. The client refused to pay the attorney anything, arguing that she had never agreed to retain the attorney and that she had received nothing of value from the attorney because the sales transaction was never concluded. Assume that there is no applicable statute or rule governing the formation of attorney-client relationships in the jurisdiction.",
        q: "If the attorney sues the client for the $500, will the attorney be likely to prevail?",
        opts: [
            "No, because even if the parties had an agreement, that agreement was discharged under the doctrine of frustration of purpose.",
            "No, because the attorney and the client never agreed on the essential terms of a contract.",
            "Yes, because the attorney took action on the client's note to his detriment.",
            "Yes, because the client's note and the attorney's performance created an implied-in-fact contract."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "(D) is the best response, because the client's note constituted an offer to have the attorney perform services, and under these circumstances the attorney's performance of the services was a reasonable method of accepting the offer.\n\nThis is a classic offer-and-acceptance problem in a non-UCC context. Therefore, let's determine (1) whether there was an offer by the client, (2) if there was an offer, whether and how the offer could be accepted; and (3) whether the lawyer in fact accepted the offer (assuming there was one) while it was still open.\n\n(1) Was there an offer? An offer is 'the manifestation of willingness to enter into a bargain, so made as to justify another person in understanding that his assent to that bargain is invited and will conclude it.' Rest. (2d) of Contracts, § 24. Keep in mind that under the objective theory of contracts (embedded in the just-quoted definition), the question is whether the would-be offeree would be justified in 'understanding' that his assent is invited, not whether the offeror subjectively intended to make an offer. Here, we know that the 'client' had a pre-existing relationship with the lawyer (since the lawyer had just finished drafting a will for her); surely when a lawyer's existing client writes the lawyer a note asking the lawyer to review the enforceability of a document the client has supplied, a reasonable person in the lawyer's position would understand that the client is making an offer to hire the lawyer to perform the work of reviewing the document and answering the question. So the client's note was unquestionably an offer to purchase services from the lawyer.\n\n(2) Next, by what means could this offer be accepted? Well, here's what Rest. (2d) § 30(2) says: 'Unless otherwise indicated by the language or the circumstances, an offer invites acceptance in any manner and by any medium reasonable in the circumstances.' Normally, it would probably be reasonable for the attorney to accept either (1) by promising to perform (e.g., by phoning or emailing the client to say, 'I'll work on it'); or (2) by actually performing (reviewing the document, and sending his letter of analysis). See Rest. 2d § 32: 'In case of doubt an offer is interpreted as inviting the offeree to accept either by promising to perform what the offer requests or by rendering the performance, as the offeree chooses.' Here, the attorney elected to render the performance, so the performance served as an acceptance as long as it amounted to a 'manner [of acceptance that was] reasonable in the circumstances.' The 'circumstances' included the fact that the client said that she 'would have talked to you in person but I'm on my way out of town [and] will be back next week.' In other words, the attorney was on notice that it would probably not be sensible to make a spoken promise of performance since the client likely wouldn't be reachable; therefore, an acceptance by performance was even more 'reasonable' than it would have been had the client been available for, say, a promise by phone call. Consequently, acceptance by performance was a valid method of acceptance on these facts.\n\n(3) Was the offer still open when it was purportedly accepted? We've already established that the attorney, by reviewing the document and sending the letter, accepted the offer by performance, assuming that the offer was still open when the attorney did this performance. Was it still open? The answer is 'yes,' because between the moment when the client sent the document and note and the moment the attorney did the work, nothing happened that would have caused the offer to terminate (e.g., a revocation, or a lapse of time).\n\nNotice that Choice (D) doesn't expressly mention either an 'offer' or an 'acceptance.' Instead, the choice says that the combination of the client's note and the attorney's performance 'created an implied-in-fact contract.' What is an 'implied-in-fact contract'? The phrase means a contract that is created not by spoken or written words but by a party's conduct. See, e.g., Farnsworth Treatise, § 3.10, p. 129: 'Sometimes a contract that results from words is described as 'express,' while one that results from conduct is described as 'implied in fact[.]'' Here, as we've seen, the attorney accepted by performing the work, so the contract's formation was the result of his 'conduct,' making Choice (D) an accurate description of why and how a contract was formed.\n\n(A) is not the best response, because there is no indication that the sales transaction's failure to be concluded was an event the non-occurrence of which was a basic assumption on which the contract was made. The doctrine of frustration of purpose is available to a party only when that party's 'principal purpose is substantially frustrated without his fault by the occurrence of an event the non-occurrence of which was a basic assumption on which the contract was made[.]' Rest. 2d, § 265. And even if this language is satisfied, frustration will still not be available if 'the language or the circumstances indicate the contrary.' Id. Here, frustration fails to apply for at least two reasons. First, the facts give us no reason to believe that the parties intended that the non-occurrence of the sale would be the sort of major event whose non-occurrence was a 'basic assumption on which the contract was made.' (For instance, suppose the lawyer advised the client that performance of the contract as written did not comply with state law; would a reasonable person in the lawyer's position really have understood that the client wouldn't have to pay for the advice because the parties' failure to close pursuant to this illegal contract would excuse the need to pay for advice that the client specifically asked for?) Second, this is one of those scenarios where 'the circumstances indicate the contrary'; when a client offers to pay a lawyer for advice about a proposed transaction, surely a reasonable person in the lawyer's position would not understand that if he did the work and gave the advice, the client could use the advice for free by claiming that the transaction's non-completion frustrated the client's purpose in contracting for the advice.\n\n(B) is not the best response, because the 'essential terms' of the contract can all be supplied by a process of reasonable implication. It certainly true that in some instances an arrangement fails to be an enforceable contract because one or more of the 'essential terms' needed for a contract have neither been expressly agreed on nor are capable of being supplied by a court with reasonable certainty. But the standard for reasonable certainty of terms is an extremely forgiving one: 'The terms of a contract are reasonably certain if they provide a basis for determining the existence of a breach and for giving an appropriate remedy.' Rest. 2d, § 33(2). Here, there would certainly be a basis for a court to determine whether the lawyer breached: if he rendered incompetent advice, or failed to render advice at all in a timely manner, these would be breaches. And if the client failed to pay anything, that, too, would clearly be a breach. As to whether the terms here 'give an appropriate remedy,' the only issue is whether a court could sensibly determine whether the $500 that the lawyer charged would be an appropriate remedy, given that neither party said anything about what the price for the service would be, or even how that price would be determined. But where the parties agree that one will perform some service for the other, the court will generally supply a price that is reasonable for that service given the circumstances. Since the facts tell us that the $500 lawyer billed 'was a reasonable fee,' the court would conclude that $500 is an appropriate remedy. Therefore, this choice is not correct in its assertion that lawyer will not recover $500 due to the parties' failure to agree on the essential terms of a contract.\n\n(C) is not the best response, because this choice implicitly refers to the doctrine of promissory estoppel, and that doctrine is not needed where, as here, the parties made a contract. This choice is asserting that the attorney is entitled to recover because he took action on the client's note, and did so to his detriment. When a recovery is premised upon the plaintiff's having taken some action in reliance on the defendant's words or acts, the recovery is typically based on the doctrine of promissory estoppel. Promissory estoppel is used (indeed, is needed) only when the parties have not made an enforceable contract; as we discuss in Choice (D), here they have made such a contract. Furthermore, when recovery is based on promissory estoppel, the usual measure of damages is reliance, not 'benefit of bargain.' The question asks you not whether the attorney is entitled to recover at all, but whether he is entitled to recover $500. The facts say that $500 is a 'reasonable fee' in the circumstances; that statement strongly suggests that whatever amount might compensate the lawyer for his 'reliance' interest, the amount would have to be substantially less than the profit-inclusive fee that a lawyer in his position would charge for the work. In other words, if the recovery were based on promissory estoppel, it would almost certainly be for a sum smaller than the $500 reasonable fee; therefore, the use of promissory estoppel wouldn't explain why 'yes' is the right answer to the question, 'If the attorney sues... for the $500, will the attorney prevail?'"
    },
    {
        id: 9,
        topic: "Remedies / Consequential Damages (Foreseeability)",
        fp: "A firm contracted with a municipality to repair a drawbridge and began work on February 1. The contract provided that the firm would be paid an additional $1,000 for each day the repair was completed before the April 1 completion deadline. On March 14, an unusual gear on the bridge broke. On that same day, the firm contracted with a supplier for a March 15 delivery to the bridge site of the only available replacement gear. The supplier did not know about the early-completion incentive-pay provision in the firm's contract. The supplier misdirected the delivery of the gear, and the firm did not receive it until March 20. The work on the bridge was completed on March 21. But for the late delivery of the gear, the firm would have completed the bridge repair on March 16.",
        q: "In an action against the supplier for breach of contract, will the firm be permitted to recover, as part of its damages, the additional $5,000 early-completion incentive pay it would have received from the municipality but for the supplier's breach?",
        opts: [
            "No, because the supplier did not expressly agree to be responsible for such damages.",
            "No, because the supplier did not know and had no reason to know about the early-completion incentive-pay provision.",
            "Yes, because the supplier is liable for all damages flowing directly from its breach.",
            "Yes, because, by the nature of its contract with the firm, the supplier should have known that time was of the essence."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "(B) is the best response, because the incentive pay was a special circumstance about which the supplier had no reason to know, making that pay not a foreseeable and probable result of the breach.\n\nThe facts of this problem are strikingly similar to those of Hadley v. Baxendale, 156 Eng. Rep. 145 (1854), probably the most famous Contracts case of all time. And if you recognized this, and remembered the outcome in Hadley, you would know immediately that Choice (B) is the correct response.\n\nWhen one party breaches a contract, the other's damages may not include 'loss that the party in breach did not have reason to foresee as a probable result of the breach when the contract was made.' Rest. 2d § 351(1). There are two types of loss that a breaching party does have reason to foresee as the probable result of a breach: (1) Loss that follows from the breach 'in the ordinary course of events'; and (2) Loss that follows from the breach 'as a result of special circumstances, beyond the ordinary course of events, that the party in breach had reason to know.' Rest. 2d, § 351(2)(a) and (b).\n\nHere, the early-completion incentive pay is certainly not the sort of provision the loss of which would follow 'in the ordinary course of events' from the supplier's five-day delay in delivery, even though that delay was clearly a breach. Rather, the loss of the pay was 'a result of special circumstances, beyond the ordinary course of events[.]' Therefore, under (2) above, the firm would be able to recover for the lost pay only if it could show that the supplier 'had reason to know' of the incentive clause. But the facts tell us that the supplier 'did not know' about the clause. And given that such clauses are relatively unusual, the supplier also did not have 'reason to know' about the clause. (Indeed, even if the supplier had 'reason to know' that construction contracts often have early-completion incentive clauses, the supplier certainly did not have reason to know that every day of delay after March 15 would not only result in loss of the incentive, but in the relatively large amount of $1,000.)\n\n(A) is not the best response, because a supplier can sometimes be responsible for an item of damages even though the supplier did not 'expressly agree to be responsible' for that type of damages. As we discuss more fully in Choice (B), a breaching party will be liable for an item of damage that is foreseeable as a probable result of a breach, even if the item is 'a result of special circumstances,' if that party either knew or had reason to know of the item. Since this choice asserts that a supplier cannot be held responsible for the incentive-pay loss based on the mere fact that the supplier never 'expressly agreed to be responsible' for the item, this choice ignores the 'knew or had reason to know' qualification to the no-recovery-for-loss-from-special-circumstances rule (discussed in Choice (B)).\n\n(C) is not the best response, because it is not the case that a breaching party is liable for 'all damages flowing directly from [that party's] breach.' As we discussed more fully in connection with Choice (B), where an item of damage flows from 'special circumstances, beyond the ordinary course of events,' the breaching party is not liable for that item (even though it flowed 'directly' from the breach), unless that party knew or had reason to know of the special circumstances. So this choice significantly overstates the damage items for which a breaching party will be liable.\n\n(D) is not the best response, because even if the supplier 'should have known that time was of the essence,' the loss stemmed from a special circumstance of which the supplier had no reason to know, making that loss not recoverable. When a contract calls for a party to perform by a certain date, treating the contract as making 'time of the essence' merely means that strict compliance with the time limit will be required, i.e., that even a small delay will probably be deemed to be a material breach (allowing the other party to cancel the contract, among other remedies). Therefore, even if the supplier knew or should have known that 'time is of the essence' (and it probably should indeed have known this), this fact would not be enough to overcome the principle, discussed in Choice (B), that damage flowing from special circumstances of which the breaching party had no reason to know is not recoverable."
    },
    {
        id: 10,
        topic: "Performance / Waiver of Express Condition",
        fp: "An architect agreed with a developer to design a large residential development. Because the architect had a history of substance abuse problems, the parties agreed that the developer's duty to accept and pay for the plans was conditioned on the architect's abstaining from drinking alcohol during the six months it would take to do the work. After two months, the architect began having several alcoholic drinks each day. The developer became aware of the architect's drinking before the architect showed the developer the preliminary plans. When the developer saw the preliminary plans, the developer told the architect that the concept was impressive and that he looked forward to seeing the final plans. The architect continued to have several alcoholic drinks each day and completed the plans within the specified contract period. However, the developer declined to review, accept, or pay for the final plans and stated that it was because of the architect's continued use of alcohol.",
        q: "The architect has sued the developer for breach of contract. Which of the following arguments best supports the architect's claim?",
        opts: [
            "The alcohol use was not a material breach of contract by the architect, since it did not affect the quality of the work.",
            "The developer waived the condition of no alcohol use.",
            "The no-alcohol term was functionally a penalty clause and therefore was unenforceable.",
            "The no-alcohol term would be interpreted as a promise and not a condition."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "(B) is the best response, because the developer, while knowing of the architect's alcohol use, nonetheless encouraged him to keep working on the plans, thereby waiving the benefit of the no-alcohol condition.\n\nWhen a contract conditions one party's duty upon the occurrence or non-occurrence of some event, that party can be found to have waived the benefit of the condition; when such a waiver is found, the waiving party (the obligor) will have to perform even though the condition has not been satisfied. Such a waiver can be either explicit or implied by the conduct of the waiving party. Generally, a court will find waiver of the condition only when the condition is a fairly minor one, i.e., one that is not a material part of the bargain. Rest. 2d, § 84(1)(a).\n\nThe developer made such an implied-by-conduct waiver here: at a time when the developer knew that the architect was not complying with the no-alcohol-use condition, the developer induced the architect to believe that the developer would not insist on compliance with the condition. The developer thus in effect promised to review, accept and pay for the plans (if the work was satisfactory) without insisting on the architect's non-use of alcohol. This implied promise also induced reliance on the architect's part: the architect continued to do the work while drinking, despite the fact that he might have been willing and able to refrain had the developer not indicated a future willingness not to insist on the condition. And since the purpose of the condition was to ensure that alcohol use would not prevent the architect from timely performing the work, the fact that the architect performed despite his alcohol use means that the condition was a minor one, not one that turned out to be needed to protect the essence of the developer's bargain.\n\nFurthermore, the non-occurrence of a minor condition is much more likely to be found to have been waived if that non-occurrence would result in a 'disproportionate forfeiture'; Rest. 2d, § 229. 'Forfeiture' in this context means 'the denial of compensation that results when the obligee loses his right to the agreed exchange after he has relied substantially, as by preparation or performance on the expectation of that exchange.' Id., Comment b. That's exactly what happened here: if the non-occurrence of the no-alcohol-use is not excused, the architect will end up having done all of the contemplated work (and done it satisfactorily), yet will be entirely denied compensation.\n\nIt's not absolutely certain that the court will apply the principles of waiver and avoidance-of-forfeiture, but there is a very good chance that the court will do so, and an argument based on waiver is clearly better than the arguments covered in the other choices.\n\n(A) is not the best response, because where (as here) a condition to a duty is an express one, strict compliance—not mere substantial compliance—will be required unless the condition's non-occurrence is excused. Some conditions are 'constructive conditions,' i.e., conditions implied by law. For instance, if the contract says that A will perform act X (which will benefit B) and B will then perform act Y, the court is likely to find as a matter of law that because acts X and Y were intended as a rough exchange, B's duty to perform Y was constructively conditioned on A's first performing X. When a condition is constructive, substantial performance suffices (i.e., strict compliance is not needed). So if the condition here had been constructive, Choice (A) would be correct, because the alcohol use would not have been a 'material breach' (i.e., would not have been a failure to substantially comply with the condition). Other conditions are 'express' ones—that is, the agreement itself either uses the word 'condition,' or otherwise makes it clear that one party is not required to perform an act unless some event occurs. The condition here is an express condition: the facts tell us that the developer's duty to accept and pay for the plans 'was conditioned on the architect's abstaining...' Where a condition is express rather than constructive, 'a rule of strict compliance traditionally applies.' Farnsworth Treatise, § 8.3, p. 510. Choice (A)—by asserting that the non-occurrence of the no-alcohol-use condition should be ignored because that non-use 'was not a material breach of contract by the architect'—ignores this general principle that strict compliance with an express condition is required. (It's only because the non-occurrence of the express condition was excused by waiver that the architect will likely be able to recover notwithstanding his alcohol use.)\n\n(C) is not the best response, because the no-alcohol term was not 'functionally a penalty clause.' In Contract law, the term 'penalty' is used to describe a clause in the contract stipulating that in the event of breach, the breaching party will be required to pay a sum of money as damages far out of proportion to the real economic loss sustained by the non-breaching party. Farnsworth Treatise, § 12.18, p. 811. If a damages provision is determined to be a penalty, it will indeed be unenforceable. But the no-alcohol term here was not (even functionally) a penalty, because it wasn't a provision determining the amount of money that would be owed (or even deducted from the contract price) in the event the architect failed to perform. Rather, the clause was part of the specification of what conduct by the architect would or would not constitute performance. And the parties are always free to define the required performance under a contract without worrying that a tough set of performance terms will be struck down as being 'functionally a penalty'; only a provision prescribing how damages are to be computed can be struck down as a penalty.\n\n(D) is not the best response, because the no-alcohol term was an express condition, and an express condition will not be interpreted as a promise. This choice hints at a valid legal principle, but misapplies it. To see this, imagine that in our question, rather than a no-alcohol-use clause, the contract said, 'Architect will deliver the finished plans by Aug. 1, and Developer will pay for them by Sept. 1 if they are merchantable.' It's unclear whether this clause means that: (1) Architect merely promises to deliver the plans by Aug. 1 (and Developer promises to pay for the plans, if merchantable, within 30 days of receipt), and the contract is silent about what effect Architect's failure to deliver the plans on time will have on Developer's subsequent obligation to pay for them; or (2) Developer's duty to pay for the plans is expressly conditioned upon architect's delivery by Aug. 1. Assume that Architect then delivers the plans on August 15, and Developer cancels the contract and refuses to ever pay anything. The choice of interpretations matters, because: if interpretation (1) is correct, then by the doctrine of constructive conditions of exchange, the court will hold that Developer could not cancel as long as the Aug. 15 delivery substantially complied with the Aug. 1 date (though Developer could claim damages for the lateness, if he could prove those damages); but if interpretation (2) is correct, then the court will apply the rule that strict compliance with express conditions is required, so that Developer was entitled to cancel the contract (and pay nothing), even if the two-week delay caused Developer no actual harm at all. In this 'was it a promise or a condition?' scenario, courts prefer interpretation 1 (a promise by Architect), since the doctrine of substantial performance spares Architect from a complete forfeiture. But the preference for interpreting the provision as a promise rather than condition applies only when the contract is ambiguous on this point; if it's clear that the parties intended to make, say, Developer's duty of payment expressly conditional on Architect's meeting the Aug. 1 delivery date (and no doctrine excuses Architect's noncompliance), then the court must interpret the provision as a condition, and must insist on strict compliance with that condition. Here, the facts say that the developer's duty to accept and pay for the plans 'was conditioned on' the architect's obeying the no-alcohol clause, making that clause an express condition. The court will therefore not be permitted to interpret the clause as a promise, as this choice suggests should be done."
    },
    {
        id: 11,
        topic: "Acceptance / Option Contracts & Death",
        fp: "A toy collector had purchased ten antique toys over the last several years and had had them restored by an expert in toy restoration. On June 1, the collector sent the 11th antique toy to the expert with a signed note that read: 'Here is another toy for you to restore. As with all prior jobs, I will pay $500 for the work, but no more.' On June 4, after receipt of the collector's June 1 note and the toy, the expert began restoring the toy. On June 6, the collector unexpectedly died. On June 7, unaware of the collector's death, the expert sent the collector a note that stated that the restoration work had begun on June 4. The following day, the expert learned of the collector's death.",
        q: "Does a contract exist that binds the expert and the collector's estate?",
        opts: [
            "No, because the collector died before the expert sent the June 7 note.",
            "No, because the offer lapsed when the collector died.",
            "Yes, because the expert sent the June 7 note before learning of the collector's death.",
            "Yes, because the offer was accepted before the collector's death."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "(D) is the best response, because when the expert began restoration on June 4, this acted as an acceptance, causing the collector's subsequent death to be too late to constitute a termination of the offer.\n\nAs with most offer-and-acceptance problems, the trick is to figure out (1) whether anything the offeree did or said could have been sufficient to constitute an acceptance (and what time that event occurred); and (2) whether the offer was still in force at the time you identified in step (1).\n\nHere, the collector's sending of the toy and the note was an offer, and that offer was ambiguous as to whether acceptance could come by promise, performance, or either. In that situation, Rest. 2d, § 32 supplies the rule: 'In case of doubt an offer is interpreted as inviting the offeree to accept either by promising to perform what the offer requests or by rendering the performance, as the offeree chooses.' Where performance takes a period of time, the beginning of performance generally constitutes acceptance. See Rest. 2d § 50, Illustr. 1 (A offers to pay B for painting A's house while A is away; when B starts to paint, he has accepted by performance.) Therefore, when the expert began restoration, that beginning acted as an acceptance (as well as a 'return promise' to complete the work; see Rest. 2d § 50(2)).\n\nSince the acceptance occurred when the expert began work on June 4, the collector's death on June 6 had no effect on the offer-and-acceptance transaction. (If the collector had died before the expert accepted by beginning work, the death would have caused the offer to terminate; see Rest. 2d § 48: 'An offeree's power of acceptance is terminated when the offeree or offeror dies[.]')\n\nBecause treating the beginning of performance as an acceptance may leave the offeror hanging (i.e., not knowing whether the offer has been accepted), acceptance by performance may require that the offeree promptly thereafter notify the offeror of the acceptance. See Rest. 2d § 54(2): 'If an offeree who accepts by rendering a performance has reason to know that the offeror has no adequate means of learning of the performance with reasonable promptness and certainty, the contractual duty of the offeror is discharged unless [with two exceptions not relevant here] (a) the offeree exercises reasonable diligence to notify the offeror of acceptance.' That's why it's significant that on June 7, the expert sent the collector a note stating that the restoration work had begun on June 4; since the expert knew that without such a note the offeror would have 'no adequate means of learning of the performance with reasonable promptness,' the collector's duty to pay for the work would have been discharged had the expert not promptly sent the note.\n\n(A) is not the best response, because prior to the collector's death, the expert had already accepted by beginning performance. This choice seems to be suggesting that nothing that happened before the expert's sending of the note on June 7 would have been an acceptance. If that suggestion were true, then this choice would be correct: as we discuss in the treatment of Choice (D), the collector's death would have automatically caused the offer to terminate. But in fact, as we cover in Choice (D), this is an offer that could be, and indeed was, accepted by the expert's beginning of performance. Once that happened, the collector's death before the expert's sending of the note on June 7 was irrelevant.\n\n(B) is not the best response, because the offer had been accepted before the collector died. As with Choice (A), if the offer had not been accepted by the moment of the collector's death, this choice, too, would have been correct—the offeror's (or the offeree's) death normally causes an offer to terminate. But as we discuss in Choice (D), the offer here had already been accepted by the expert's beginning of performance.\n\n(C) is not the best response, because the expert had already accepted the contract by beginning performance, and his sending of the June 7 note served only the function of avoiding discharge, a function that would have been served even if the expert had already learned of the collector's death before sending the note. As we discuss in Choice (D), the offer was accepted by the expert's beginning of performance. It's true that, again as discussed in Choice (D), given that the collector had no reason to know of this acceptance, the expert was required to give prompt notice of the acceptance in order to avoid the collector's being discharged from his obligation. But although the expert was required to send the note reasonably promptly after beginning performance, there was no requirement that the note be sent before the expert learned of the collector's death (because as we discuss in the last part of the treatment of Choice (D), the note served to avoid a discharge of a completed contract, not to accept a still-outstanding offer). Since this choice incorrectly asserts that it was only because the expert sent the June 7 note 'before learning of the collector's death,' the choice gives an incorrect explanation for the correct outcome (that a contract exists that binds the estate and the expert)."
    },
    {
        id: 12,
        topic: "Terms / Parol Evidence Rule (Consistent Additional Terms)",
        fp: "A contractor agreed to remodel a homeowner's garage for $5,000. Just before the parties signed the one-page written contract, the homeowner called to the contractor's attention the fact that the contract did not specify a time of completion. The parties orally agreed but did not specify in the contract that the contractor would complete the work in 60 days, and then they both signed the contract. The contract did not contain a merger clause. The contractor failed to finish the work in 60 days. The homeowner has sued the contractor for breach of contract.",
        q: "Is the court likely to admit evidence concerning the parties' oral agreement that the work would be completed in 60 days?",
        opts: [
            "No, because the court must ascertain the meaning of the agreement from the terms of the written contract.",
            "No, because the oral agreement was merely part of the parties' negotiations.",
            "Yes, because the contract is ambiguous.",
            "Yes, because the time limit is an additional term that does not contradict the partially integrated written contract."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "(D) is the best response, because where a written contract is only partially integrated, the parol evidence rule does not prevent proof of a prior orally-agreed-upon term that is an 'addition to' (rather than a contradiction of) the writing.\n\nThis problem presents a classic illustration why, in applying the parol evidence rule, it's vital to determine whether the written agreement is 'partially integrated' or 'totally integrated.' To start, let's review the difference between the two types of integrated written agreements. An 'integrated agreement' (whether total or partial) is 'a writing or writings constituting a final expression of one or more terms of an agreement.' Rest. 2d § 209(1). (Since 'final expression' is part of this definition, the phrase 'final integration' is redundant.) Now, then, what's the difference between a 'totally integrated' agreement and a 'partially integrated' agreement? Here's how the Second Restatement defines the two terms:\n\n§ 210 Completely and Partially Integrated Agreements\n(1) A completely integrated agreement is an integrated agreement adopted by the parties as a complete and exclusive statement of [all] the terms of the agreement.\n(2) A partially integrated agreement is an integrated agreement other than a completely integrated agreement....\n\nTo summarize, once you determine that a writing is a 'final expression' of at least one term of an agreement, then you know that (1) Total integration: if the writing was intended by the parties as a 'complete and exclusive' statement of every one of the terms of the agreement (i.e., was intended to displace all prior oral or written statements of any term in the agreement), you know that the writing is a 'complete integration'; and (2) Partial integration: if there is at least one term that was the subject of a prior written or oral understanding that the present writing was not intended to displace, then the current writing is merely a 'partial integration.' (By the way, whether an integrated agreement is completely or partially integrated is a matter 'to be determined by the court as a question preliminary to determination of a question of interpretation or to application of the parol evidence rule.' Rest. 2d, § 210(3).)\n\nLet's apply this test to the one-page writing we have here. The facts refer to the document as 'the' one-page written contract, and suggest that when the parties signed it, they did not intend to execute a later writing. This is strong circumstantial evidence that the one-page document was an 'integrated' agreement (i.e., that it was intended to be a 'final expression' of at least some terms of the 'deal,' such as the price and, probably, the scope of the work). But was the writing a 'total' integration or just a 'partial' one? The facts tell us that the writing did not specify a time for completion, and that just before the parties signed it, they orally agreed on a 60-day time. Just from the absence of a time for completion (clearly a part of the parties' intended 'deal'), we therefore know that the writing can't be a complete integration, because that absence means that the writing was not 'a complete and exclusive statement of [all] the terms of the agreement.'\n\nNow that we've determined that the writing was a partially integrated (not totally integrated) manifestation of the agreement, we're ready to see what the parol evidence rule says about whether the prior oral agreement as to time-for-completion can be considered as evidence of what the parties intended. Here's the Restatement's formulation of the rule; you'll see that the rule prescribes a very different effect depending on whether the writing is a total or a partial integration:\n\n§ 213 Effect of Integrated Agreement on Prior Agreements (Parol Evidence Rule)\n(1) A binding integrated agreement [whether partially or totally integrated] discharges prior agreements to the extent that it is inconsistent with them.\n(2) A binding completely integrated agreement discharges prior agreements to the extent that they are within its scope.\n\nHere's the practical effect of this distinction between partial and total integrations: (1) If the prior term which a party is trying to prove (whether oral or written) is 'inconsistent' with a term in the integrated writing (i.e., contradicts a term in the writing), then it doesn't matter whether the integration is complete or partial: the proof of the prior term cannot come in in either case. (2) But if the prior term the party is trying to prove (whether oral or written) is not inconsistent with any term in the integrated writing, then that prior term—which by hypothesis is an 'additional' term rather than a contradiction of a term in the integrated writing—(i) may be proved if the integrated writing is partial, but (ii) may not be proved if the integrated writing is complete.\n\nHere, the prior oral time-for-delivery term is not 'inconsistent' with any term in the one-page written contract, since the facts tell us that the writing 'did not specify a time of completion.' Since we determined above that the writing is a partial rather than complete integration, we now know that the time-for-delivery term may be proved, since it is an addition to (rather than inconsistent with) the terms of the writing. This choice, by saying that the term limit may be proved because it is 'an additional term that does not contradict the partially integrated written contract,' exactly states the principle explained above.\n\n(A) is not the best response, because the court may allow proof of this consistent additional term, since the writing is only partially integrated. There is no general rule that a court must 'ascertain the meaning of [a written contract] from the terms of [that] contract,' as this choice seems to assert. Even if the writing here were totally integrated (i.e., was intended as a complete and exclusive statement of the terms of the agreement), the court would still be able to interpret ambiguous terms in the writing by looking to the parties' negotiations, prior course of dealing, etc. Here, where the writing is just a partial integration, this choice is even further from being correct, since the court may also consult evidence of the parties' oral agreement even though the evidence is offered to prove a consistent additional term, rather than merely to interpret an ambiguous term in the writing.\n\n(B) is not the best response, because the oral agreement represented the parties' actual agreement as to the time for completion—the oral agreement was not 'merely part of the parties' negotiations.' The facts say that when the parties discussed the time for completion before signing the document, they 'orally agreed' to the 60-day term; the facts are therefore inconsistent with this choice's assertion that what the parties said orally was 'merely part of the parties' negotiations.' If the oral discussion of completion time had happened before the document was drafted, the contractor would have had a better chance of succeeding in the claim that time-of-completion was never truly agreed on (though even in that situation, the court would probably conclude that there was an 'agreement,' not merely a 'negotiation' on this point.) But since the oral discussion happened after the document was drafted, it's implausible for the court to find that the discussion was merely a negotiation.\n\n(C) is not the best response, because a time-for-completion clause is simply missing from the document, not worded in an ambiguous way. When a writing, even one that the court determines to have been intended as a total integration, uses a term ambiguously, evidence of the parties' prior writings or oral discussions may be looked to by the court to help it interpret the meaning of that term. But that's not what the homeowner is trying to achieve here—he or she is trying to add an additional term to the writing, on the theory that the parties agreed to it before signing; the owner is not merely trying to present evidence of their discussion in order to resolve an ambiguity in the writing's language. Therefore, this choice correctly predicts the outcome, but does so based on an incorrect legal analysis of why the evidence will be admissible."
    },
    {
        id: 13,
        topic: "Defenses / Illegality (Revenue-Raising vs Regulatory)",
        fp: "In order to raise revenue, a city required home-repair contractors who performed work within the city limits to pay a licensing fee to a city agency. A contractor who was unaware of the fee requirement agreed to perform home repairs for a city resident. After the contractor completed the work, the resident discovered that the contractor had not paid the licensing fee, and she refused to pay for the repairs, which were otherwise satisfactory.",
        q: "If the contractor sues the resident for breach of contract, how is the court likely to rule?",
        opts: [
            "Although the contract violates the law and is void, the court will require the homeowner to pay the contractor the reasonable value of the work accepted.",
            "Although the contract violates the law, the court will find that public policy does not bar enforcement of the contract, because the purpose of the fee is merely to raise revenue.",
            "Because the contract violates the law and is void, the court will not enforce it.",
            "Because the purpose of the fee is merely to raise revenue, the court will find that the contract does not violate the law but will allow the contractor to recover his costs only."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "(B) is the best response, because where a licensure requirement is designed merely to raise money, and a provider has rendered services without complying with the requirement, a court will not refuse to enforce the agreement on public policy grounds.\n\nContrary to what you may think you remember from law school, there is no general doctrine to the effect that where performance of a contract would violate the law (i.e., the 'contract would be illegal'), the court will refuse to enforce that contract. Instead, the only relevant doctrine is that certain agreements will be 'unenforceable on grounds of public policy.' See, e.g., Rest. 2d, § 178(1): § 178 When a Term Is Unenforceable on Grounds of Public Policy. (1) A promise or other term of an agreement is unenforceable on grounds of public policy if legislation provides that it is unenforceable or the interest in its enforcement is clearly outweighed in the circumstances by a public policy against the enforcement of such terms. Since the facts here give us no reason to believe that the ordinance establishing the licensing fee explicitly provides that a contract by a home-repair contractor who has not paid the license fee will be unenforceable, the contractor's right to recover on the contract here will be nullified only if the second clause of the above rule—that 'the interest in [the agreement's] enforcement is clearly outweighed in the circumstances by a public policy against the enforcement of such [an agreement]' is found to apply.\n\nBecause there are many cases involving the argument that the court should not enforce a services contract where the service provider is not licensed to do the work covered by the contract, the Second Restatement contains a special provision to deal with this situation: § 181 Effect of Failure to Comply with Licensing or Similar Requirement. If a party is prohibited from doing an act because of his failure to comply with a licensing, registration or similar requirement, a promise in consideration of his doing that act or of his promise to do it is unenforceable on grounds of public policy if (a) the requirement has a regulatory purpose, and (b) the interest in the enforcement of the promise is clearly outweighed by the public policy behind the requirement. So unless the licensing fee here has a 'regulatory purpose,' we will never even get to the question of whether the general interest in enforcing contracts is outweighed by the 'public policy behind' the licensing requirement. Here, we're told that the provision is a 'licensing fee,' and we're told nothing to suggest that this fee is part of an attempt by the city to regulate how home-repair contractors perform their services. Therefore, the court will probably hold that the fee does not have a 'regulatory purpose' (just a revenue-raising purpose); in that event, the court will not refuse to enforce the agreement public policy grounds. See, e.g., Farnsworth Treatise, § 5.6, p. 340, discussing the licensing cases, and saying that 'A court will not, however, regard the policy underlying a requirement designed merely to raise revenue as sufficiently strong to justify a refusal [to enforce the agreement].'\n\n(A) is not the best response, because the contract is not void. The term 'void contract' is really an oxymoron. The term is used to describe '[a] promise for breach of which the law neither gives a remedy nor otherwise recognizes a duty of performance by the promisor[.]' Rest. 2d, § 7, Comment a. But such a 'void contract' 'is not a contract at all.' Id. If this choice had said that the contract was 'voidable,' the choice would at least avoid using the nonsensical term 'void contract.' But even then, as we discuss in Choice (B), the fact that the license-fee requirement was designed solely to raise revenue would prevent the court from finding that the contract was voidable. In reality, as we discuss in Choice (B), the court will enforce the contract—it will require the homeowner to pay the contract price, not merely 'the reasonable value of the work accepted' (which would be a reasonable remedy if the court found that the contractor's failure to pay the fee made the contract voidable by the homeowner, and thus prevented the contractor from recovering on the contract).\n\n(C) is not the best response, because the contract is not void. As we discuss regarding Choice (A), the contract here is not void. Indeed, it is not even 'voidable'—as we discuss in Choice (B), because the licensing-fee requirement has a purpose of raising revenue, not regulation, the court will enforce the contract according to its terms.\n\n(D) is not the best response, because the court will allow the contractor to recover on the contract, not merely to recoup his costs. If the licensing provision here had a major regulatory purpose—so major that a court would conclude that the societal interest in enforcement of contracts was outweighed by the purpose of enforcing the regulatory mechanism—then this answer might well be correct. In that situation, the court would essentially be treating the contract as 'voidable,' but would likely allow the contractor to recover at least his costs—a non-contract type of recovery—in order to avoid an unfair forfeiture. But since, as we describe in Choice (B), the contract is fully enforceable notwithstanding the contractor's failure to pay the fee, the contractor will be allowed to recover 'on the contract,' i.e., to recover the contract price."
    },
    {
        id: 14,
        topic: "Third Parties / Third-Party Beneficiaries",
        fp: "Collection of a debtor's $2,000 debt to a creditor was barred by the applicable statute of limitations. The debtor sold and delivered his car to a buyer under a written agreement, signed by the buyer, in which the buyer promised to pay the $2,000 purchase price to the creditor 'in satisfaction of [the debtor's] debt to [the creditor].'",
        q: "Can the creditor recover the $2,000 from the buyer?",
        opts: [
            "No, because payment of the $2,000 to the creditor would undermine the statutory public policy against enforcement of stale claims.",
            "No, because the creditor's rights as an intended beneficiary are subject to any defenses available to the contracting parties between themselves.",
            "Yes, because the buyer's promise to pay $2,000 to the creditor is enforceable by the creditor regardless of whether the debtor was legally obligated to pay the creditor anything.",
            "Yes, because the buyer's promise to pay $2,000 to the creditor revived the uncollectible debt."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because the (1) the creditor is an intended beneficiary of the buyer's promise to pay $2,000 to the creditor; and (2) the buyer's promise would be interpreted as a promise to pay the specified amount (the $2,000) regardless of whether the debtor was legally obligated to pay that $2000 to the creditor.\n\nThis is a third-party beneficiary problem: you're asked whether the beneficiary (the creditor) may recover against the promisor (the buyer) for the $2,000 that the promisor has promised the promisee (the debtor) that the promisor will pay to the beneficiary (the creditor). The first question you have to answer is, was the beneficiary an 'intended' beneficiary or an 'incidental' beneficiary (to use the Second Restatement's phrasing)? You need to decide this because only an intended beneficiary—not an incidental beneficiary—may sue the promisor for enforcement of the promise. Let's look at how Rest. 2d, § 302 defines intended beneficiaries:\n\n§ 302 Intended and Incidental Beneficiaries\n(1) Unless otherwise agreed between promisor and promisee, a beneficiary of a promise is an intended beneficiary if recognition of a right to performance in the beneficiary is appropriate to effectuate the intention of the parties and either\n(a) the performance of the promise will satisfy an obligation of the promisee to pay money to the beneficiary; or\n(b) the circumstances indicate that the promisee intends to give the beneficiary the benefit of the promised performance\n(2) An incidental beneficiary is a beneficiary who is not an intended beneficiary.\n\nHere, § 302 makes the creditor an intended beneficiary, because: (1) recognizing a 'right to performance' on the part of the creditor is 'appropriate to effectuate the intention of the parties' (§ 302(1)); and (2) the performance of the promise by the buyer (to pay the $2,000 to the creditor) 'will satisfy an obligation of the promisee [the debtor] to pay money to the beneficiary [the creditor][.]' (§ 302(1)(a).)\n\nHowever, there's a second issue you also have to resolve: does the fact that the debtor (the promisee) would have a valid defense if the creditor sued him for $2,000 mean that the buyer (the promisor) may also raise this valid defense now that the creditor is suing the buyer on a third-party beneficiary theory? This really becomes an issue of interpreting the intent of the parties to the debtor-buyer contract: we need to answer the question, was the buyer promising the debtor (1) 'I will pay $2,000 to the creditor regardless of whether you really in fact owe $2,000 to the creditor,' or merely (2) 'I will pay the $2,000 if and only if your debt of $2,000 to the creditor would be found to be valid and enforceable if the creditor were to sue you on it'? It's highly likely that the court would pick interpretation (1): the debtor seems to be interested in making sure that the creditor receives $2,000 regardless of whether this debt is really owing. (For instance, the debtor may merely want to avoid stiffing the creditor, or may want to clear up his own credit rating despite the unenforceability of the debt.) Once the court picks interpretation (1), then the buyer will not be permitted to raise against the creditor the statute of limitations even though the debtor could have raised the statute if he were the one that the creditor was suing. See Rest. 2d, § 309, Comment c: '[T]he beneficiary's right against the promisor is not subject to claims and defenses of the promisee against the beneficiary unless the contract [between the promisor and the promisee] so provides.' (Here, if, as is very likely, the court chooses interpretation (1), that will be tantamount to a conclusion by the court that the contract 'does not so provide,' i.e., that the promisor-promisee contract does not provide that the beneficiary's rights against the promisor/buyer will be subject to defenses that the promisee/debtor could raise against the beneficiary.)\n\n(A) is not the best response, because (1) there is no 'statutory public policy against enforcement of stale claims'; and (2) in any event, the creditor, as an intended beneficiary of the buyer's promise to pay the $2,000, may enforce that promise. First, there is no significant 'statutory public policy against enforcement of stale claims.' It's true that every jurisdiction has statutes of limitation. But it's not accurate to say that these embody a serious policy against enforcing 'stale claims'—the policy behind statutes of limitation is one of procedural fairness to the defendant (since with the passage of time witnesses' memories fade, documents are lost, etc.), but there is no societal interest in ensuring that stale claims are not enforced. (That's why states enforce signed promises to forgo statutes of limitations.) Second, as we discuss in Choice (C), the rules governing intended beneficiaries explicitly provide that where the promisor promises the promisee to pay the latter's debt to a creditor, this promise is enforceable by the creditor against the promisor even if the debt is time-barred. So even if there were a serious 'public policy against enforcement of stale claims,' that policy would be outweighed by the policy of allowing intended beneficiaries to enforce promises to pay specific time-barred claims like the one here.\n\n(B) is not the best response, because there are no relevant 'defenses available to the contracting parties between themselves.' When this choice refers to 'the contracting parties,' it is presumably referring to the debtor and the buyer (who have contracted for the buyer to pay the $2,000 to the creditor). If the buyer had a viable claim against the debtor for material breach of the buyer-debtor contract (e.g., because the condition of the car was not as warranted by the debtor), then the buyer probably would be able to assert this claim as a defense to the creditor/beneficiary's suit against him. See Rest. 2d, § 309. But the only defense that's possibly relevant here is the debtor's defense against the creditor, and that would not be a 'defense available to the contracting parties [the buyer and the debtor] between themselves.' In any event, as we discuss in Choice (C), the buyer's promise to pay $2,000 to the creditor would be interpreted as an enforceable promise to make that payment whether or not the debtor's debt in that amount to the creditor would really be enforceable, so this choice does not even state the correct outcome, let alone the correct reasoning.\n\n(D) is not the best response, because the reason the creditor can recover is not because the buyer's promise to pay the $2,000 'revived the uncollectible debt.' As we discuss in Choice (C), the buyer's promise to pay $2,000 to the creditor would be interpreted as an enforceable promise to make that payment whether or not the debtor's debt in that amount to the creditor would really be enforceable in a suit brought by the creditor against the debtor. In other words, the buyer's promise is enforceable without any need for that promise to 'revive the uncollectible debt.' And, in fact, the buyer's promise would not revive the uncollectible debt itself—for instance, if the creditor sued the debtor (instead of suing the buyer) for the $2,000, the creditor's suit would be time-barred, just as if the buyer had never made the promise."
    },
    {
        id: 15,
        topic: "Excuses / Impracticability (Illness)",
        fp: "An engineer signed a two-year contract to serve as the chief safety engineer for a coal mine at a salary of $7,000 per month. The position required the engineer to work underground each workday. After one week on the job, the engineer became very ill. He requested testing of the mine air system, which revealed the presence of a chemical agent to which the engineer had a rare allergic reaction. The engineer promptly quit. The coal mine then hired a qualified replacement for the remainder of the engineer's contract at a salary of $7,500 per month. Assume that no statute or regulation applies.",
        q: "If the coal mine sues the engineer for breach of contract, is the coal mine likely to recover damages?",
        opts: [
            "No, because an at-will employee has the right to terminate an employment contract.",
            "No, because the risk to the engineer's health excused his nonperformance of the contract.",
            "Yes, because the coal mine acted in good faith in mitigating the effect of the engineer's failure to finish the contract term.",
            "Yes, because the mine is reasonably safe for most people."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "(B) is the best response, because the engineer's allergy made it impracticable for him to perform the contract without undergoing a health risk disproportionate to the benefits to the mine from having that engineer rather than a substitute complete the contract.\n\nThis question presents a classic illustration of the principle that 'supervening impracticability' may act to discharge a party from its duties under a contract. Let's look at how the Second Restatement explains the supervening impracticability principle: § 261 Discharge by Supervening Impracticability. Where, after a contract is made, a party's performance is made impracticable without his fault by the occurrence of an event the non-occurrence of which was a basic assumption on which the contract was made, his duty to render that performance is discharged, unless the language or the circumstances indicate the contrary. Since the contract here calls for personal services that can be performed only by the engineer, any event that would render the engineer's performance impracticable is an 'event the non-occurrence of which was a basic assumption on which the contract was made.' Notice that all that is required is that the party's performance be 'impracticable,' not that it be 'impossible.' Comment d to § 261 says that one of the ways that performance may be impracticable is 'because it will involve a risk of injury to person or property, of one of the parties or of others, that is disproportionate to the ends to be attained by performance.' That is certainly the case here—had the engineer remained on the job once the chemical agent to which he had a rare allergy was revealed, he would presumably once again have 'become very ill.' Especially given the ready availability of a 'qualified replacement' for slightly more money, we can confidently say that the 'risk of injury' to the engineer from continuing to perform was disproportionate to the benefits to the mine of having this engineer rather than the substitute complete the work. And nothing in the 'language or the circumstances' surrounding the employment agreement 'indicate[s] the contrary,' i.e., indicates that the engineer should not be discharged. Therefore, the engineer will be discharged under the doctrine of impracticability, and this discharge will prevent the coal mine from recovering anything. By the way, another provision in the Second Restatement deals expressly with when the 'death or incapacity of a person necessary for performance' will cause a discharge for impracticability. § 262 says, 'If the existence of a particular person is necessary for the performance of a duty, his death or such incapacity as makes performance impracticable is an event the non-occurrence of which was a basic assumption on which the contract was made.' Since the engineer's serious allergy made his performance impracticable, § 262 tells us that that incapacity automatically constitutes 'an event the non-occurrence of which was a basic assumption on which the contract was made.'\n\n(A) is not the best response, because this contract had a two-year term, so the employee was not 'at-will.' If the contract had truly been 'at will,' this would be the correct answer. But when we say a contract is 'at will,' we mean that it does not have a stated term or duration—it can be terminated by either party at any time for virtually any reason. However, the facts tell you that this is a 'two-year contract'; this means that neither party may terminate before two years without some sort of excuse, breach by the other party, or other special event.\n\n(C) is not the best response, because the risk to the engineer's health caused his duties under the contract to be discharged by virtue of the doctrine of impracticability. As we discuss at length regarding Choice (B), the risk to the engineer of another severe allergic reaction was the type of event the non-occurrence of which was a basic assumption on which the contract was made. Therefore, the occurrence of that risk caused the engineer's continued performance to be a duty that was discharged under the doctrine of impracticability. That being the case, the engineer did not breach the agreement, and the coal mine cannot recover for breach despite the fact that it acted reasonably in trying to mitigate its damages.\n\n(D) is not the best response, because the fact that the mine is reasonably safe for most people does not prevent discharge of the engineer's contractual responsibility by virtue of the doctrine of impracticability. As we discuss regarding Choice (B), the risk to the engineer of another severe allergic reaction was the type of event the non-occurrence of which was a basic assumption on which the contract was made. That risk therefore caused the engineer's duties under the contract to be discharged by virtue of the doctrine of impracticability. The fact that the risk to the engineer was idiosyncratic (not shared by most people) is irrelevant; for this particular person with duties under the contract, performance would involve disproportionate risks, triggering the doctrine of impracticability."
    },
    {
        id: 16,
        topic: "Defenses / Fraud (Non-Disclosure)",
        fp: "Before putting her home up for sale, a homeowner painted the living room ceiling to conceal major water damage caused by a leaking roof that had not yet been repaired. On the first day the home was offered for sale, the homeowner gave a buyer a personal tour. The homeowner made no statements at all regarding the water damage or the roof. Without discovering the water damage or the leaking roof and without consulting a lawyer, the buyer immediately agreed in writing to buy the home for $200,000.\n\nBefore the closing date, the buyer discovered the water damage and the leaking roof. The cost of repair was estimated at $22,000. The buyer has refused to go through with the purchase.",
        q: "If the homeowner sues the buyer for breach of contract, is the homeowner likely to prevail?",
        opts: [
            "No, because no contract was formed since the buyer did not have a real opportunity to understand the essential terms of the contract.",
            "No, because the homeowner concealed evidence of the water damage and of the leaking roof.",
            "Yes, because the homeowner made no affirmative statements of fact about the water damage or the leaking roof.",
            "Yes, because the buyer acted unreasonably by failing to employ an inspector to conduct an independent inspection of the home."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "(B) is the best response, because it expresses the reason for which the buyer is entitled to rescind the contract.\n\nIf one party's assent to a contract is induced by a material misrepresentation by the other, on which the recipient is justified in relying, the contract is voidable by the recipient. Rest. 2d of Contracts § 164(1). Normally, a party's silent failure to mention a fact will not constitute a misrepresentation as to that fact. But intentional acts of concealment are deemed to be equivalent to misrepresentations. See Rest. 2d § 160: 'Action intended or known to be likely to prevent another from learning a fact is equivalent to an assertion that the fact does not exist.' So when the homeowner painted the ceiling for the purpose of concealing the water damage, this was affirmative conduct intended to prevent the buyer from learning about the damage, and therefore was the equivalent of an affirmative statement that there was no water damage. Consequently, the buyer is entitled to avoid the contract for misrepresentation, either in his own action to rescind the contract, or (as here) as a defense to the homeowner's damage action.\n\n(A) is not the best response, because it is not factually or legally correct. Factually, there is no evidence that the buyer did not 'understand the essential terms' of the contract. Furthermore, even if the buyer didn't understand the essential terms, this fact would not necessarily be grounds for the buyer to avoid the contract—for instance, if the buyer failed to understand the essential terms because he was given the contract and failed to read it closely, this would not entitle him to avoid the contract. The real problem here is misrepresentation.\n\n(C) is not the best response, because the lack of an affirmative misstatement in words is not dispositive. It's true that, normally, A's right to avoid a contract on account of B's misrepresentation requires that B have made an explicit misrepresentation, and B's mere failure to disclose a known fact will not constitute a misrepresentation. But where B takes a physical act for the purpose of concealing a fact, the act is considered to be the equivalent of an affirmative representation that the fact does not exist. (See the fuller discussion of this principle in Choice (B).) So the homeowner, by painting over the water damage for the purpose of concealing it, will be treated as having in effect made a representation that there was no such damage, triggering the buyer's right to avoid for misrepresentation.\n\n(D) is not the best response, because the buyer's failure to inspect does not make a legal difference to the outcome. When one party makes a misrepresentation that induces the other to enter a contract, the latter does not lose the right to avoid the contract for misrepresentation merely because he failed to take advantage of an opportunity to investigate. See Rest. 2d § 172, Comment b (in case of a misrepresentation, 'The recipient is generally entitled to rely on the maker's assertions... without undertaking an investigation as to their truthfulness.'). So even though the buyer had the opportunity to employ an inspector, his failure to do so did not affect his right to rescind for misrepresentation. (As discussed in Choice (B), the homeowner's concealment of the damage was equivalent to a misrepresentation that no such damage existed.)"
    },
    {
        id: 17,
        topic: "Remedies / Foreseeability",
        fp: "An experienced rancher contracted to harvest his neighbor's wheat crop for $1,000 'when the crop [was] ripe.' In early September, the neighbor told the rancher that the crop was ripe. The rancher delayed because he had other customers to attend to. The neighbor was concerned that the delay might cause the crop to be lost, for hailstorms were common in that part of the country in the fall. In fact, in early October, before the crop was harvested, it was destroyed by a hailstorm.",
        q: "Is the rancher liable for the loss?",
        opts: [
            "No, because no time for performance was established in the contract.",
            "No, because the neighbor failed to tell the rancher that the crop might be destroyed by a hailstorm.",
            "Yes, because at the time the contract was made, the rancher had reason to foresee the loss as a probable result of his breach.",
            "Yes, because a party who undertakes a contractual obligation is liable for all the consequences that flow from his breach."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because the foreseeability of the loss is what makes the loss recoverable.\n\nThis question involves application of an aspect of the Hadley v. Baxendale, 156 Eng. Rep. 145 (1854), doctrine. As the Restatement puts the general Hadley principle, 'Damages are not recoverable for loss that the party in breach did not have reason to foresee as a probable result of the breach when the contract was made.' Rest. 2d of Contracts § 351(1). That section goes on to specify the two situations in which loss will be deemed foreseeable. One of these two situations is where the loss follows from the breach 'as a result of special circumstances, beyond the ordinary course of events, that the party in breach had reason to know.' Section 351(2) (b). This 'had reason to know of special circumstances' provision applies here. That's because (1) we're told that the rancher was 'experienced' (and the matters in which he's experienced include the local conditions, since what he's agreed to harvest is his neighbor's crop, not some far-away crop); and (2) we're told that autumn hailstorms are 'common' in that area of the country. So the fact that the neighbor didn't expressly warn the rancher of the hailstorm danger doesn't matter—the rancher still had 'reason to know of the special circumstances.'\n\n(A) is not the best response, because the contract did establish a time for performance. It's true that the contract did not specify a particular date for the harvest. But the clause saying that the work was to be done 'when the crop [was] ripe' gave sufficient guidance about the time for performance.\n\n(B) is not the best response, because it cites a fact that is not dispositive. It's true that under the doctrine of Hadley Baxendale, supra, the non-breaching party may not recover losses that the breaching party did not have reason to foresee, as of the time the contract was made, would be a probable result of breach. Rest. 2d of Contracts § 351(1). But there are multiple ways in which the breaching party may have reason to foresee a particular type of loss, and a warning from the non-breaching party is only one of these. Thus, Rest. § 351(2) (b) imposes liability for losses stemming from breach 'as a result of special circumstances, beyond the ordinary course of events, that the party in breach had reason to know.' And the breaching party may have 'reason to know' of the special circumstances even without a warning from the non-breaching party. As is discussed in more detail in Choice (C), here the rancher, because of his experience, had reason to know about the hailstorm danger, even though the neighbor didn't warn him of it.\n\n(D) is not the best response, because it overstates the scope of a breaching party's liability. It's true that normally, the breaching party will be liable for the consequences flowing from his breach. But under the doctrine of Hadley v. Baxendale, supra, the non-breaching party may not recover losses that the breaching party did not have reason to foresee, as of the time the contract was made, would be a probable result of breach. Rest. 2d of Contracts § 351(1). This choice, by indicating that a contracting party is liable for 'all the consequences' of his breach, overstates such a party's liability. So even though this choice reaches the correct outcome (rancher is liable), it does so by overly broad reasoning."
    },
    {
        id: 18,
        topic: "Formation / Intent to Memorialize",
        fp: "A niece had worked in her aunt's bookstore for many years. The bookstore business, which was housed in a building that the aunt leased, was independently appraised at $200,000. The aunt decided to retire. She wrote to the niece, expressing her affection for the niece and offering to sell her the bookstore business for $125,000 if the landlord would agree to a transfer of the lease. The letter also specified when the aunt would transfer the business. The niece wrote back accepting her aunt's offer. In a phone call to the niece, the aunt stated that the landlord had approved the transfer of the lease and that she would now ask her attorney to draft a written contract so that there would be a record of the terms. Before the attorney had finished drafting the document, the aunt changed her mind about selling the business and informed the niece of her decision.",
        q: "In an action for breach of contract brought by the niece against her aunt, is the niece likely to prevail?",
        opts: [
            "No, because the motivation for the transfer of the business was the aunt's affection for her niece, not the price.",
            "No, because the promised consideration was inadequate in light of the market value of the business.",
            "Yes, because the condition concerning the landlord's assent to the transfer of the lease was beyond the control of either party.",
            "Yes, because the document being drafted by the attorney was merely a record of an agreement already made, not a condition to it."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "(D) is the best response, because it correctly indicates that the original informal agreement is enforceable.\n\nIf the parties reach (perhaps orally) what would otherwise be a binding agreement, the fact that they further agree that the contract will be embodied in a not-yet-drafted writing does not prevent the original agreement from being binding. And that's true even if the anticipated writing is never prepared. See Rest. 2d of Contracts § 27: 'Manifestations of assent that are in themselves sufficient to conclude a contract will not be prevented from so operating by the fact that the parties also manifest an intention to prepare and adopt a written memorial thereof; but the circumstances may show that the agreements are preliminary negotiations.' Here the very detailed back-and-forth discussions between the parties indicate that the parties intended to be bound as of the time of the phone call; there is nothing in the circumstances to show that the 'manifestations of assent' were merely 'preliminary negotiations.'\n\n(A) is not the best response, because the presence of a discounted price does not affect the enforceability of the agreement. If the aunt had promised to make a true gift of the business to the niece, the absence of consideration would prevent the promise from being enforceable. But as long as there is some sort of a bargain, the fact that one party was in part motivated by affection for the other party does not prevent consideration from existing. See Rest. 2d of Contracts § 79, Comment c: 'Ordinarily... courts do not inquire into the adequacy of consideration.... [This principle] is also applied even when it is clear that the transaction is a mixture of bargain and gift.' If the discount from market value had been so great that it indicated that there was merely a pretense of a bargain (e.g., sale of a $200,000 business for $2), the promise would be unenforceable for lack of consideration. But the 37 percent discount here is not nearly great enough to indicate the total absence of a bargained-for exchange, so the consideration requirement was satisfied.\n\n(B) is not the best response, because the court will not inquire into the relative equivalence between the business's market value and the price agreed upon. Ordinarily, courts do not inquire into whether the values being exchanged are even approximately equal—that's what is meant by the (correct) saying that 'courts don't inquire into the adequacy of the consideration.' See Rest. 2d of Contracts § 79: 'If the requirement of consideration is met, there is no additional requirement of... (b) equivalence in the values exchanged[.]' The aunt clearly bargained for the exchange of her business for a $125,000 payment, and that bargain was enough to satisfy the consideration requirement, even though the market value of what the aunt gave up was greater than the value of what she received. If the discount from market value had been so great that it indicated that there was merely a pretense of a bargain (e.g., a 99.9 percent discount), that fact might demonstrate a lack of consideration, which would make the agreement unenforceable. But the discount here is not nearly great enough to indicate the total absence of a bargained-for exchange.\n\n(C) is not the best response, because it cites a legally-irrelevant fact. It's true that the effectiveness of the deal proposed by the aunt's original offer was conditional on the landlord's approval. But the fact that this condition (which was not within the control of either party) had not yet been satisfied when the niece wrote her letter did not prevent that letter from acting as an acceptance. Binding contracts are often entered into but made subject to the fulfillment of a later condition that is not within the control of either party. Once the landlord approved, the condition was satisfied, and the aunt lost her right to undo the arrangement."
    },
    {
        id: 19,
        topic: "Interpretation / Express Terms vs. Trade Usage",
        fp: "An actor straight out of drama school and an agent entered into a one-year written contract that described the services the agent would provide. Because he was eager for work, the actor agreed, in the contract, to pay the agent 15 percent of his yearly earnings. At the end of the year, the actor was so pleased with his many roles that he gave the agent 20 percent of his earnings. After the first contract had expired, the actor and the agent decided to continue working together. They photocopied their old contract, changed the date, and signed it. At the end of the year, a dispute arose as to what percentage of earnings the actor owed. It is a trade practice in the acting profession for actors to pay their agents 10 percent of their yearly earnings, payable at the end of the year.",
        q: "What percentage of the actor's earnings is a court most likely to award the agent?",
        opts: [
            "20 percent, because course of dealing is given greater weight than trade usage.",
            "15 percent, because it was an express term of the contract.",
            "10 percent, because trade usage is the applicable default rule.",
            "Nothing, because the contract is too indefinite."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "(B) is the best response, because express terms control over terms of usage.\n\nFirst, notice that this is not a contract governed by the UCC, which contains very specific rules about priority among express terms, trade usage, etc. But in non-UCC cases, courts tend to follow the same priority rules as used in the UCC. Those rules attach the most weight to express terms. See Rest. 2d of Contracts § 203(b): 'In the interpretation of a promise or agreement or a term thereof, the following standards of preference are generally applicable:... (b) express terms are given greater weight than course of performance, course of dealing, and usage of trade[.]' Since the first contract contained the 15 percent figure as an express term, and since the parties photocopied that contract to make the template for the present contract, the 15 percent figure became an express term of the present contract. Therefore, that 15 percent express term prevails over any alternative meaning, including course of dealing (which probably doesn't apply anyway; see the discussion of Choice (A) below) or trade usage.\n\n(A) is not the best response, because no course of dealing has been established here, and even if it had been, it would have yielded to the express term. A 'course of dealing' is 'a sequence of previous conduct between the parties to an agreement which is fairly to be regarded as establishing a common basis of understanding for interpreting their expressions and other conduct.' Rest. 2d of Contracts § 223(1). As the reference to 'sequence' indicates, a single prior instance of conduct would not suffice to create a course of dealing. And there's nothing to indicate that the parties were 'establishing a common basis of understanding' that future payments would also be 20 percent. Furthermore, since the parties photocopied the prior agreement, and the prior agreement contained the 15 percent clause, the 15 percent became an express term, which would have given that term priority even if the 20 percent was a course of dealing (which it wasn't).\n\n(C) is not the best response, because trade usage would not be controlling usage on these facts. As among express terms, course of performance, course of dealing, and trade usage, trade usage has the lowest (not highest) priority. Rest. 2d of Contracts § 203(b). So if the case involves either an express term, a course of performance or a course of dealing, the presence of a trade usage on the same issue will be irrelevant. That's the case here, since the parties' use of the first agreement as a template meant that the express 15 percent term from that first agreement became an express term in the new contract.\n\n(D) is not the best response, because the contract has enough detail to avoid being too indefinite. It's true that even though the parties have intended to contract, their agreement will be unenforceable if its terms are unduly indefinite. Or, as the Restatement puts it, to be enforceable the contract's terms must be 'reasonably certain.' Rest. 2d of Contracts § 33(1). The terms are 'reasonably certain' if they 'provide a basis for determining the existence of a breach and for giving an appropriate remedy.' Id. at § 33(2). This is a fairly easy-to-satisfy requirement, and it is certainly met here. The writing specifies the duration, describes the services to be rendered, and sets forth the method of computing the agent's fee (15 percent of the actor's earnings); those details are sufficient to allow a court to determine whether the agent has performed the specified services, and to calculate damages if the actor breaches by not paying."
    },
    {
        id: 20,
        topic: "Remedies / Consequential Damages in Loan Contracts",
        fp: "A bank agreed to lend a merchant $10,000 for one year at 8 percent interest. The loan proceeds were to be disbursed within two weeks. The merchant intended to use the loan proceeds to purchase a specific shipment of carpets for resale at an expected profit of $5,000 but said nothing about these plans to the bank. The bank failed to disburse the proceeds and refused to assure the merchant that it would do so. The merchant was able to secure a loan from another lender at 10 percent interest for one year. However, by the time the merchant started the application process for a substitute loan, it was too late to pursue the opportunity to buy the shipment of carpets.",
        q: "In an action against the bank for breach of contract, which of the following amounts is the merchant likely to recover?",
        opts: [
            "Nothing, because lost opportunities are not foreseeable.",
            "Nothing, because the parties failed to tacitly agree that the merchant would be entitled to damages in the event of a breach by the bank.",
            "The difference in cost over time between a loan at 10 percent and a loan at 8 percent.",
            "$5,000, the merchant's foreseeable loss."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because it correctly states the limits on a borrower's right to recover damages for breach of a loan commitment.\n\nThe non-breaching party's right to recover damages is limited by the doctrine of Hadley, Baxendale, 156 Eng. Rep. 145 ((1854). As the Hadley doctrine is articulated by the Restatement, 'Damages are not recoverable for loss that the party in breach did not have reason to foresee as a probable result of the breach when the contract was made.' Rest. 2d of Contracts § 351. In the case of a contract to lend money, the lender is usually entitled to presume that an alternative source of loan money is readily available, unless the borrower has brought home to the lender at the time of the loan contract the borrower's likely inability to borrow elsewhere. Nothing in these facts indicates that the merchant gave the bank reason to believe that the merchant wouldn't be able to promptly borrow elsewhere. Furthermore, the merchant didn't mention to the bank the entire proposed carpet-resale transaction that the merchant intended, so the bank had no idea of the probability that these profits would be lost if the bank breached. So for two different reasons, the damages from the lost resale profits are ones that the bank 'did not have reason to foresee as a probable result of the breach.' On the other hand, it was reasonably foreseeable to the bank that if it breached, the best available alternative lender might charge a higher rate of interest, so the extra interest cost to the merchant is recoverable.\n\n(A) is not the best response, because, although the statement it makes is often correct, this choice does not deal with the 'extra interest cost' problem. It is true that often, the value of a 'lost opportunity' to make a profit will be found to be too speculative to be recovered, or will alternatively be found to be the sort of special damage that is not recoverable because the breaching party was not aware of the likelihood that it would occur in the event of breach. In the case of a contract to lend money, the lender is ordinarily entitled to presume that the borrower will find an alternative source of funding, so that no loss of opportunity will occur in the event the lender breaches. That's the case here. On the other hand, the lender should reasonably expect that an alternative loan would cost a higher rate of interest, so the lender will be responsible for these increased interest costs. Since this choice does not contemplate recovery of the increased interest costs, it's not the best choice.\n\n(B) is not the best response, because courts reject the tacit agreement test. There was a time when some courts held that a party should not be held liable for consequential damages unless, at the time he made the contract, he was found to have made a 'tacit agreement' to assume the risk of that liability. Farnsworth, § 12.14. But modern courts (and the UCC, by the way) have generally rejected the tacit-agreement test. Id. Therefore, if there is no evidence either way about whether the parties intended the non-breaching party to be able to recover consequential damages, that party is entitled to do so (subject, of course, to the foreseeability limitations of Hadley v. Baxendale, supra).\n\n(D) is not the best response, because the $5,000 loss was not a 'foreseeable loss.' Under the principle of Hadley v. Baxendale, supra, the breaching party is responsible only for those types of damages that, as of the making of the contract, he had reason to foresee as a likely result of breach. In the case of a contract to lend money, the lender is usually entitled to presume that an alternative source of loan money will be readily available, unless the borrower has brought home to the lender at the time of the loan contract the borrower's likely inability to borrow elsewhere. Nothing in these facts indicates that the merchant gave the bank reason to believe that the merchant wouldn't be able to promptly borrow elsewhere. Furthermore, the merchant didn't mention to the bank the entire proposed carpet-resale transaction, so the bank had no reason to foresee that the resale profits would be lost if the bank breached. Therefore, the $5,000 in lost profits was, for two different reasons, not foreseeable to the bank, preventing the bank from being liable for the merchant's loss of those profits."
    },
    {
        id: 21,
        topic: "Third-Party Beneficiaries / Incidental Beneficiaries",
        fp: "A janitorial service contracted in writing with a hospital for a one-year term. Under the terms of the contract, the janitorial service agreed to clean the hospital daily in accordance with the hygiene standards of the city's health code. Because the janitorial service did not clean a patient's room in accordance with the required hygiene standards, the patient contracted an infection that required continued hospitalization. In addition to suing the hospital, the patient sued the janitorial service for breach of contract.",
        q: "Which of the following statements is most accurate with respect to the breach of contract claim against the janitorial service?",
        opts: [
            "The janitorial service is liable to the patient as a matter of public policy, because it violated the city's health code.",
            "The patient is an intended third-party beneficiary under the contract, because the janitorial service's promise was intended to benefit all hospital patients.",
            "The patient has no claim for breach of contract against the janitorial service, because she is an incidental beneficiary.",
            "The patient cannot sue on the contract, because she was not named in the contract."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "(C) is the best response, because there is no affirmative evidence that the hospital intended the contract to benefit hospital patients.\n\nSince the patient was not a party to the contract between the janitorial service and the hospital, she would have to recover, if at all, on a third-party-beneficiary theory. A non-party who is found to be an 'intended beneficiary' of the contract may recover, but a non-party who is found to be merely an 'incidental beneficiary' may not recover. According to the Restatement and the prevailing modern view, the decision whether the beneficiary was intended or incidental is to be made principally by looking at the intent of the promisee (here, the hospital). See, e.g., Rest. 2d of Contracts § 302(b): putting aside the creditor-beneficiary scenario (not present here), the beneficiary is an intended one only if 'the circumstances indicate that the promisee intends to give the beneficiary the benefit of the promised performance.' So, unless there is affirmative evidence that the hospital intended to give its patients the benefit of the promise (an intent that would presumably have to include an intent to give the patient the right to sue the janitorial service if it didn't perform), each patient would merely be an incidental beneficiary.\n\nHere, the only circumstances we're told about suggest that the hospital was mainly or exclusively concerned with fulfilling its duty of hygiene under the city's health code, not with giving patients a right of action if the janitorial service did not perform. While the question is a close one—there is a plausible argument that the hospital had a secondary desire to make sure that its patients benefited from hygienic rooms—the slightly-better prediction-of-outcome is the one made in this choice. (In any event, we can say that this is the choice that the National Conference of Bar Examiners, the drafters of this question, say is the right answer.)\n\n(A) is not the best response, because it is not a legally-correct statement. As between the original parties to the contract, the fact that the janitorial service violated the health code would demonstrate a breach by the service, since it expressly contracted to do the cleaning in the manner specified by the health code. But in a suit by a stranger to the contract, it would not be enough for the plaintiff to show a breach—the plaintiff would have to show that, even though she was a non-party, she is entitled to sue as an intended beneficiary under the law of third-party beneficiaries. Since this choice does not refer to the rules governing third-party beneficiaries, it cannot be the best choice.\n\n(B) is not the best response, because it draws a factual assertion that is not justified by the statement of facts. If the circumstances justified the conclusion that the hospital, in making the contract, intended to supply a benefit to all hospital patients, this would be the correct answer—each patient would be an 'intended' beneficiary (rather than an 'incidental' one), and intended beneficiaries may sue for breach. But for the reasons discussed more fully in Choice (C), a court would be (slightly) more likely to conclude that there is insufficient evidence that the hospital had a conscious intent to benefit patients, as opposed to an intent to satisfy the requirements of the health code.\n\n(D) is not the best response, because it overlooks the possibility that an unnamed person can nonetheless be an intended beneficiary. It simply is not correct to state, as this choice does, that a party who is 'not named in the contract' is automatically stripped of the right to sue. A person may be an 'intended beneficiary' even though she is not specifically named in the contract, if the circumstances are such that it can be inferred that the promisee intended to benefit either that person or members of a class to which that person belonged. See Rest. 2d of Contracts § 308: 'It is not essential to the creation of a right in an intended beneficiary that he be identified when a contract containing the promise is made.' So, for instance, if there were affirmative evidence that the hospital intended to benefit all of its patients, the fact that this particular patient was not named would not be fatal to her third-party-beneficiary claim. The problem, rather, is that there is (probably) not sufficient evidence that the hospital intended to benefit patients as a class when it made the contract."
    },
    {
        id: 22,
        topic: "Defenses / Fraudulent Misrepresentation",
        fp: "A developer contracted in writing to sell to a buyer a house on a one-acre lot for $100,000. The developer told the buyer that the lot abutted a national park and that the water for the house came from a natural artesian spring. The developer knew that both of these representations were important to the buyer and that both were false. The buyer moved into the house and eight months later learned that a private golf course was being constructed on the adjacent land and that the water for his house was piped in from the city reservoir. The buyer immediately sued the developer to avoid the contract. The construction of the golf course will probably increase the market value of the buyer's property, and the water from the city reservoir exceeds all established standards for drinking water.",
        q: "Is the buyer likely to prevail?",
        opts: [
            "No, because eight months exceeds a reasonable time for contract avoidance.",
            "No, because the developer's misstatements caused no economic harm to the buyer.",
            "Yes, because the contract was void ab initio.",
            "Yes, because the buyer retained the power to avoid the contract due to fraud."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "(D) is the best response, because a person who is induced to enter a contract by means of a fraudulent misrepresentation has the power to avoid the contract.\n\nWhere one party induces the other to enter into a contract by making a fraudulent or material misrepresentation, the latter may avoid the contract. Thus the Restatement says that 'If a party's manifestation of assent is induced by either a fraudulent or a material misrepresentation by the other party upon which the recipient is justified in relying, the contract is voidable by the recipient.' Rest. 2d of Contracts § 164(1). Here, all the required elements are present: (1) We know that the buyer was 'induced' by the misrepresentations, because we are told that these representations 'were important' to the buyer. (It's not necessary that the misrepresentations have been the 'but for' cause of the plaintiff's decision to enter the contract; all that's required is that the misrepresentation 'substantially contributed to the plaintiff's decision. Rest. 2d § 167.) (2) We know that the representations here were fraudulent, because we're told that the developer knew that they were false. (Therefore, under the Restatement test the misrepresentations do not even have to be 'material,' though the ones here pretty clearly are material.) (3) The buyer was 'justified' in relying on the statements, because the case does not involve any of the special situations that pose problems of the justifiability of reliance (e.g., certain assertions of opinion, assertions as to matters of law, etc.), and there is nothing in the facts to indicate that the buyer was foolish to rely on the assertions. So the buyer was entitled to avoid the contract for fraud. The fact that the buyer did not suffer pecuniary harm is irrelevant, as is discussed in Choice (B).\n\n(A) is not the best response, because the buyer acted promptly upon learning of the statements' falsity. It's true that where a person is fraudulently induced to enter a contract, that person's power of avoidance can be lost by undue delay. However, the 'avoidance clock' does not start to run until the time when the victim learns of the fraud. See Rest. 2d of Contracts § 381(2): 'The power of a party to avoid a contract for misrepresentation or mistake is lost if after he knows of a fraudulent misrepresentation he does not within a reasonable time manifest to the other party his intention to avoid it.' Since the buyer here sued for avoidance 'immediately' upon learning of the fraud, the fact that eight months elapsed between the fraud and the suit doesn't matter.\n\n(B) is not the best response, because economic harm is not a requirement for an action to avoid on account of fraud. The requirements for avoidance of a contract for fraud are as stated in the discussion of Choice (D). These requirements do not include a requirement that the victim have suffered economic harm. In fact, the person seeking avoidance does not need to show any sort of harm at all, economic or otherwise. See Rest. 2d § 164, Comment c.\n\n(C) is not the best response, because the contract was not void, merely voidable. 'Void ab initio' means 'void from the outset.' Where a party is induced to make a contract on account of a misrepresentation that meets the requirements stated above, the contract is merely 'voidable' at the victim's option, not 'void from the outset.' So, for instance, if the buyer here had decided that he wanted to go through with the contract, the developer would not have been permitted to escape the contract by claiming that it was void ab initio."
    },
    {
        id: 23,
        topic: "Performance / Implied Duty of Good Faith",
        fp: "A seller entered into a contract to sell to a buyer a house for a price of $150,000. The contract contained the following clause: 'This contract is conditional on the buyer's securing bank financing at an interest rate of 7% or below.' The buyer did not make an application for bank financing and therefore did not secure it, and refused to proceed with the purchase. The seller sued the buyer for breach of contract.",
        q: "Is the seller likely to prevail?",
        opts: [
            "No, because the buyer did not secure bank financing.",
            "No, because the contract did not expressly impose on the buyer any obligation to apply for bank financing.",
            "Yes, because a court will excuse the condition to avoid a disproportionate forfeiture.",
            "Yes, because a court will imply a term imposing on the buyer a duty to use reasonable efforts to secure bank financing."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "(D) is the best response, because the buyer lost the right to rely on non-occurrence of the condition due to his failure to make reasonable efforts to obtain the financing.\n\nTo begin with, 'Every contract imposes upon each party a duty of good faith and fair dealing in its performance and its enforcement.' Rest. 2d of Contracts § 205. Where a duty of one party is subject to the occurrence of a condition and that party's cooperation is necessary for the condition to occur, a court will typically conclude that such cooperation is part of that party's duty of good faith. See Rest. 2d § 245, Comment a. That's the case here: The buyer's duty was conditional upon receipt of financing, and that financing could not occur without the cooperation of the buyer (by his making an application). So the court will find an implied term requiring the buyer to make a good-faith application for the financing. Since the buyer did not fulfill this duty to make good-faith efforts, he has breached. Furthermore, 'Where a party's breach by non-performance contributes materially to the non-occurrence of a condition of one of his duties, the non-occurrence is excused.' Rest. 2d § 245. See also Illustr. 3 to § 245, which is exactly on point (buyer's failure to make mortgage application causes him to lose the benefit of the mortgage-financing contingency in the contract). So when the buyer failed to make reasonable efforts to get financing, he lost the right to assert the lack of bank financing as the non-occurrence of a condition to his duty to close.\n\n(A) is not the best response, because it ignores the significance of the buyer's failure to make good faith efforts to get the financing. A performance that is subject to an express condition cannot become due unless the condition is either satisfied or excused. Here, because the buyer will be held to have owed a duty to make good-faith efforts to obtain the financing, his lack of such efforts will cause the condition to be excused. The problem with this choice is that it ignores the fact that the lack of financing would be excused due to the buyer's conduct.\n\n(B) is not the best response, because even though no obligation to apply for the financing was expressly stated in the contract, the buyer had an implied obligation to apply for it. As is more extensively discussed in Choice (D), every contract contains an implicit obligation of good faith. Where a party's duty is conditional upon the occurrence of an event, that party's duty of good faith will typically be held to include an implied obligation to make reasonable efforts to see that the condition is satisfied. That's the case here. So even though the contract did not expressly impose on the buyer an obligation to seek bank financing, the contract will be found to contain an implied obligation on the buyer's part to do so.\n\n(C) is not the best response, because it reaches the right result based on incorrect legal reasoning. It's true that courts can and often do excuse the non-occurrence of a condition in order to avoid forfeitures. See, e.g., Rest. 2d § 229: 'To the extent that the non-occurrence of a condition would cause disproportionate forfeiture, a court may excuse the non-occurrence of that condition unless its occurrence was a material part of the agreed exchange.' But here, excusing the non-occurrence of the financing condition would not subject the seller to any significant forfeiture—there's no indication that depriving the seller of the right to insist on a closing would cause him any disproportionate loss. (He can sell the house to someone else, presumably for about the same price.) The non-occurrence of the financing condition will indeed be excused, but this will happen because the buyer breached his implied duty to try to satisfy the condition (by applying for financing), not because the court's refusal to excuse the condition would cause the seller to suffer a forfeiture."
    },
    {
        id: 24,
        topic: "Third-Party Beneficiaries / Delegation",
        fp: "A computer retail outlet contracted to service a bank's computer equipment for one year at a fixed monthly fee under a contract that was silent as to assignment or delegation by either party. Three months later, the retail outlet sold the service portion of its business to an experienced and well-financed computer service company. The only provision in the agreement between the retail outlet and the computer service company relating to the outlet's contract with the bank stated that the outlet 'hereby assigns all of its computer service contracts to [the computer service company].'\n\nThe computer service company performed the monthly maintenance required under the service contract. Its performance was defective, however, and caused damage to the bank's operations.",
        q: "Whom can the bank sue for damages arising from the computer service company's defective performance?",
        opts: [
            "The retail outlet only, because the computer service company made no promises to the bank.",
            "Either the retail outlet or the computer service company, because the bank has not released the outlet and the bank is an intended beneficiary of the outlet's agreement with the computer service company.",
            "Either the retail outlet or the computer service company, because since each has the right to enforce the bank's performance of its contract with the retail outlet, mutuality of remedy renders either potentially liable for the defective performance.",
            "The computer service company only, because it is a qualified and a financially responsible supplier of computer services."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "(B) is the best response, because a delegation does not release the delegator, and the delegate is deemed to have promised to perform the delegated services.\n\nFirst, let's look at whether the retail outlet may be sued. The outlet effectively delegated to the computer service company the outlet's duty to perform for the bank. This happened automatically when the outlet said that it 'assigns all of its computer service contracts[.]' That's because an assignment 'of the contract' automatically includes a delegation of duties. See Rest. 2d § 328(1): 'Unless the language or the circumstances indicate the contrary... an assignment of 'the contract' or of 'all my rights under the contract' or an assignment in similar general terms is an assignment of the assignor's rights and a delegation of his unperformed duties under the contract.' But a delegation of duties does not, unless the obligee expressly agrees, release the assignor/delegator. See Rest. 2d § 318(3): 'Unless the obligee agrees otherwise, neither delegation of performance nor a contract to assume the duty made with the obligor by the person delegated discharges any duty or liability of the delegating obligor.' So even though the outlet successfully delegated its duties, and the service company assumed them, the outlet remained liable to the bank, because the bank did not specifically agree to release the outlet from these liabilities.\n\nNow, let's look at whether the service company is liable. When a contracting party assigns 'the contract' to an assignee, and the assignee accepts the assignment, the assignee is deemed to have promised to perform the assignor's duties, and the obligee is an intended beneficiary of that implied promise. Rest. 2d § 328(2). So when the outlet assigned all of its service contracts, and the service company accepted the assignment, the service company was deemed to have made a promise to perform the outlet's not-yet-performed services under those contracts, and the bank was deemed to be an intended beneficiary of that promise of performance by the service company. Since an intended beneficiary of a promise may sue the promisor for breach, the bank may recover against the service company.\n\n(A) is not the best response, because it incorrectly concludes that the service company made no promise to the bank. As is discussed more fully in Choice (B), when the service company accepted the assignment of the contracts, it was deemed to have promised to perform the assignor's (the outlet's) duties under those contracts, and the obligee (the bank) was an intended beneficiary of that promise.\n\n(C) is not the best response, because the retail outlet lost its right to enforce the bank's performance, and in any event there is no relevant doctrine of 'mutuality of remedy' applicable here. First, this choice is not correct in saying that the retail outlet still has the right to enforce the bank's performance of the contract. The outlet lost its right to enforce performance when it made the assignment. See Rest. 2d § 317(1): 'An assignment of a right is a manifestation of the assignor's intention to transfer it by virtue of which the assignor's right to performance by the obligor is extinguished in whole or in part and the assignee acquires a right to such performance.' Second, even as to the rights and obligations of the service company, it is not because the service company is liable to the bank that the bank therefore gets the right to sue the service company by virtue of 'mutuality of remedy'—the reason the bank can recover against the service company is because the bank is an intended beneficiary of the service company's implied promise to the outlet that it would perform the outlet's remaining duties.\n\n(D) is not the best response, because it incorrectly concludes that the outlet does not remain liable. When an existing party with remaining obligations makes an assignment and delegation, that party remains liable if the assignee/delegate doesn't perform. The fact that the assignee/delegate is qualified and financially responsible is part of what makes the contract assignable in the first place, but this fact does nothing to change the general rule that an assignment does not release the assignor from liability to the obligee, except in the special situation where the obligee expressly agrees to such a release (which didn't happen here)."
    },
    {
        id: 25,
        topic: "Defenses / Undue Influence",
        fp: "The mother of a son and a daughter was dying. The daughter visited her mother in a hospice facility and said, 'You know that I have always been the good child, and my brother has always been the bad child. Even so, you have left your property in the will to us fifty-fifty. But it would be really nice if you would sell me the family home for $100,000.'\n\n'I don't know,' said the mother. 'It is worth a lot more than that at least $250,000.'\n\n'That is true,' said the daughter. 'But I have always been good and visited you, and my brother has never visited you, so that ought to be worth something. And besides, if you won't sell me the house for that price, maybe I won't visit you anymore, either.'\n\n'Oh, I wouldn't want that,' said the mother, and she signed a contract selling the house to her daughter for $100,000.\n\nShortly thereafter, the mother died. When her son found out that the house had been sold and was not part of his mother's estate, he sued to have the contract avoided on behalf of the mother.",
        q: "On what ground would the contract most likely be avoided?",
        opts: [
            "Duress.",
            "Inadequate consideration.",
            "Mistake.",
            "Undue influence."
        ],
        ans: 3,
        exp: "(D) is the best response, because the daughter used her domination of the mother in order to unfairly persuade her to make the sale.\n\nOne of the grounds for avoiding a contract is that it has been induced by undue influence. Undue influence exists where one party to the contract is subjected to 'unfair persuasion,' which can stem either from the 'domination' of the victim by the persuader, or from the fact that the victim is 'by virtue of the relation between [the victim and the persuader]... justified in assuming that [the persuader] will not act in a manner inconsistent with [the victim's] welfare.' Rest. 2d of Contracts § 177(1). Here, both sources of unfair persuasion were arguably present: (1) the mother's illness, together with the daughter's threat not to visit her when the mother was vulnerable, allowed the daughter to 'dominate' the mother; and (2) the mother-daughter relationship justified the mother in assuming that the daughter would not act in a way inconsistent with the mother's welfare (welfare that arguably included treating the two children equally). It's not certain that the undue influence ground will succeed, but it is the only one that has a reasonable prospect of success.\n\n(A) is not the best response, because duress requires an improper threat. Duress in inducing a contract is certainly a ground for avoiding a contract. However, duress as a grounds for avoidance will be found to exist only where the victim's agreement to make the contract was 'induced by an improper threat by the other party that leaves the victim no reasonable alternative.' Rest. 2d § 175(1). Various types of threats may be 'improper,' including, inter alia, threats to commit what would be a crime or tort, threats to bring a criminal prosecution, and threats to do something that is a 'use of power for illegitimate ends.' Rest. 2d § 176. But the only threat made here was the daughter's threat not to visit her mother anymore. And that threat, while inconsiderate, would probably not be deemed sufficiently 'improper.' (Also, the threat was not so coercive as to leave the mother with 'no reasonable alternative.')\n\n(B) is not the best response, because a bargained-for exchange was present. As is often said, 'Courts do not inquire into the adequacy of the consideration.' That is, as long as there is a real bargain, the fact that the two things being exchanged are of materially different market value does not prevent consideration from being present. Therefore, a sale for less than market value, brought about by a mixture of bargain and gift, will not reflect a lack of consideration.\n\n(C) is not the best response, because no mistake was present here. A contract can indeed be avoided for either mutual or unilateral mistake, under the right circumstances. However, a 'mistake' is defined as 'a belief that is not in accord with the facts.' Rest. 2d § 151. There is no indication here that the mother's decision to accede to the sale request was brought about by any belief on the mother's part that was not in accordance with the facts."
    }
];